        when "en"
          [
            rule_section(:table_aim, "Mahjong: aim of the game",
              "In the default Solitaire game you remove matching pairs and win by clearing the layout. Hong Kong, Riichi and MCR are separate games against other players: you build your own winning hand and score points. Each has its own complete part below, starting with its aim and terms.",
              "Sounds distinguish dealing, drawing and discarding a tile, declaring a set and ending a hand. Solitaire also sounds pair removal and undo. Passing and browsing tiles are silent. Spoken descriptions still identify the tile and result.",
              "While responding to a tile, D reads the tile to which Pass, Chow, Pung, Kong or Mahjong applies. It does not change or submit your choice. In Solitaire, D still reads coordinates.",
              "In Hong Kong, Riichi and MCR, W also reads a subtotal of basic bonuses already present: dragon and scoring-wind triplets, plus the applicable bonus tiles or an accepted Riichi declaration. Concealed triplets count while you keep them together. This is not the value of an unfinished hand: whole-hand patterns may replace basic bonuses. In MCR, flowers are listed separately and do not help reach 8 fan; Riichi lists visible dora separately because dora require yaku. When creating a table, Basic scoring preview under W defaults to Yes; choose No to keep only the legal-win readout. The setting never changes scoring or permission to win. MCR: 2/3/4 concealed pungs = 2/16/64 fan; these awards replace one another. W uses a concise subtotal while incomplete and the full legal-win value when available.",
              "After a win, Hand value lists the scoring components actually awarded, excluding replaced patterns. In MCR it separates the hand fan from flower/season extras and states their total for payment; extras never count towards the 8-fan qualifying minimum."),
            rule_section(:aim, "Solitaire \u2014 terms and aim",
              "Remove all 144 tiles in matching pairs. You play alone and win when you remove the last pair.",
              "In Solitaire, the layout is the whole arrangement of tiles. A pair is two matching pieces; a layer is a height level; rows run across and columns down. A free tile has nothing above it and at least one open side, left or right. A visible tile can still be blocked on both sides.",
              "You do not build chows, pungs or kongs, or count fan, han or yaku in this variant. Those belong to the multiplayer games. Here you win by removing every pair from the layout."),
            rule_section(:tiles, "Solitaire: Matching and free tiles",
              "A pair normally contains two tiles with the same suit and number, the same wind, or the same dragon. Any two flowers match each other, as do any two seasons. A flower does not match a season.",
              "Both tiles must be free: no tile may overlap them from above, and each must have an open left or right side. A visible tile can still be blocked on both sides. Covered tiles are announced without revealing their faces."),
            rule_section(:play, "Solitaire: Choosing a pair",
              "In Tiles in the layout, Left and Right move between columns; Up and Down move between rows. Press 1, 2 or 3 to switch layers in Terraces; Flat has only layer 1. A layer is a height level, not a row. Press D to hear the layer, row and column, counted separately within each layer.",
              "Each visible tile is announced as available from the left, from the right, from both sides, or blocked on both sides. A covered tile is announced without its face. Empty positions remain after removal, so the coordinates of the other tiles do not change.",
              "Find a free tile and press Enter to mark it. Then find another free matching tile and press Enter to remove both. The first selection alone removes nothing. A mismatched second tile leaves the first selected. Press Enter again on the first tile, or Escape, to cancel the selection. The game does not list matching pairs for you.",
              "Removing a pair can free tiles underneath or beside it. Consider which tiles your choice will uncover. A new puzzle has a solution, but some choices can leave you without another matching pair.",
              "Example: find a free bamboo 5 and press Enter to mark it. Find another free bamboo 5, possibly on another layer, and press Enter. Both are removed. If the second 5 is covered or blocked, first free it by removing other pairs.",
              "When several identical tiles are available, consider which copies to remove. Removing an edge tile may free its neighbour; removing an upper tile uncovers one below. Not every available pair leads to a solution. Undo the last pair lets you return and try another order."),
            rule_section(:ending, "Solitaire: Undo, progress and finishing",
              "Use Undo the last pair to restore the most recently removed tiles. You can undo several moves, one pair at a time, until the starting layout returns.",
              "Progress shows how many pairs you have removed and how many tiles remain. Your score is the number of removed pairs, up to 72. When no pair is available, undo a move to try another route or choose End this puzzle. Ending early does not count as clearing the layout.",
              "S reads your removed-pair score. M reads how many tiles remain in the layout. A new puzzle has 144 tiles; removing one pair leaves 142."),
            rule_section(:solitaire_points, "Solitaire \u2014 points for removing pairs",
              "Solitaire uses no fan, han or fu. The score is the number of removed pairs: each pair scores 1, and undoing its removal subtracts that point. Clear all 144 tiles, or 72 pairs, to win."),
            rule_section(:layout, "Solitaire: Layout choice",
              "Terraces is the default layered layout. Flat places all tiles on one layer. The matching rule and the requirement for a free left or right side are the same in both layouts."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_aim, "Hong Kong \u2014 Aim and winning conditions",
              "Four players compete to complete a winning hand first. The normal shape is four sets and a pair, and it must be worth at least 3 fan: fan measure the hand\u2019s scoring value. Over a full match, the player with the highest total points wins. The terms below explain the sets and scoring units before your first turn."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_terms, "Hong Kong \u2014 How the terms work",
              "You play against the other players. Tiles in your hand are yours to arrange into a winning pattern; you do not remove matching pairs from a layout as in Solitaire. A hand is one deal and its play. A match may contain many hands.",
              "- A tile is one piece, such as bamboo 5. Its kind is its suit and number, or its particular wind or dragon. There are four copies of each ordinary kind.",
              "- Suits are characters, bamboo and circles, numbered 1\u20139. Different suits do not mix in one sequence. Terminals are 1 and 9; simples are 2\u20138.",
              "- Honors are East, South, West and North winds, and white, green and red dragons. They have no number and cannot form a sequence.",
              "- A chow or chi is three consecutive numbers in one suit: bamboo 3, 4 and 5. Bamboo 3, circles 4 and bamboo 5 do not qualify. Nor do 8, 9 and 1 of one suit.",
              "- A pung, also written pong or pon, is three identical tiles, such as three red dragons. Three fives from different suits are not a pung.",
              "- A kong or kan is four identical tiles. You must declare it through the kong action; simply holding four copies does not declare a kong. It is one set, with an extra replacement draw.",
              "- A pair is two identical tiles. A set is a chow, pung or kong. An ordinary winning hand needs four sets and one pair, not merely several pairs or a large number of tiles of one suit.",
              "- The wall is the supply of tiles you draw from. Drawing adds a tile to your hand; discarding removes one tile from your hand and offers it to opponents.",
              "- A closed hand has no sets claimed from opponents. Claiming a chow, pung or exposed kong opens it. Sets formed entirely from your own tiles can stay concealed. A declared concealed kong keeps your hand closed.",
              "- A self-draw win uses the tile you have just drawn. A discard win uses an opponent\u2019s discarded tile to finish your hand. Claim Mahjong to win; do not discard the winning tile first.",
              "- A wait is a tile kind that would finish your hand. With bamboo 4\u20135 you might wait for bamboo 3 or 6. Being one tile from a complete shape is called tenpai. A complete shape must still satisfy this variant\u2019s scoring conditions.",
              "- Fan are scoring units awarded for patterns and winning circumstances. You need at least 3 fan to win this profile. They are converted into match points; 3 fan does not mean you gain only 3 points.",
              "- Your seat wind is your current role: the dealer is East, followed by South, West and North. The round wind belongs to the whole table. A pung of your own wind or the round wind can score; a pair of that wind is not enough for that award.",
              "- Flowers and seasons are bonus tiles. The game exposes and replaces them automatically. They do not fill a chow, pung or the pair and are separate from wind rounds."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_start, "Hong Kong \u2014 Starting the hand",
              "Balances start at zero; winning hands add payments and losing them subtracts payments.",
              "The dealer starts with 14 tiles and discards first. Everyone else starts with 13. On a normal turn, choose Draw a tile and press Enter. Drawing is an action; moving through the tile list does not draw."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_play, "Hong Kong \u2014 Your first turns",
              "Before your first discard, read your hand with Up and Down. Think of numbered tiles as possible sequences, identical tiles as possible pungs, and one pair as your eventual pair. You do not select all four sets in the interface: sets left in your hand are recognised when you declare a win.",
              "While you browse game fields, a new turn focuses Draw a tile. After drawing, focus moves to the newly drawn tile in your hand, marked just drawn. This does not discard it. You can move to another tile with the arrows or keep the new tile and discard an old one.",
              "To discard, find one tile in the available-to-discard list and press Enter. You lose that copy from your hand. If you hold several identical copies, discarding one does not remove the others.",
              "After another player discards, listen to the tile and read the response list. Pass means you do not claim that tile; it does not end your participation or concede the hand. If everyone passes, the next player draws. Passing several times does not mean you must draw each time: it may still be another player\u2019s turn.",
              "The first field describes the current situation. All available actions follow it: responses, Draw, Declare Mahjong, kong choices or Riichi when applicable. Your tile lists come after those actions. Tab and Shift+Tab move between fields; arrows choose within a list, and Enter confirms. An unavailable action is omitted.",
              "Read Exposed sets to inspect everyone\u2019s declared sets; each entry names its owner. Discards records which tiles each player has discarded. These fields help you see what is already used and what opponents may need. You can review them without submitting a move.",
              "If a tile completes an eligible winning hand, choose Declare Mahjong rather than discarding it or passing. The game checks both the shape and this variant\u2019s scoring requirements. If the wall runs out first, the hand is a draw; nobody wins merely for having the most nearly complete hand.",
              "Press S to hear the current points of every player. Press M to hear how many tiles remain in the wall for drawing. These are read-only announcements: they do not draw, discard or pass, and do not reveal other hands.",
              "W reads a legal winning hand value: fan or han and fu, and the points gained. Without a legal win it does not give a final hand value. Before a Riichi win it excludes hidden ura-dora, so the final value may be higher. U reads your exposed sets, Shift+U those of the other players, and R the hand number, winds and current situation. In Riichi, I reads dora indicators and tiles, your dora count, Riichi declarations, deposits, honba and your furiten restriction. These keys only read information."),
            rule_section(:table_hand, "Hong Kong \u2014 What you must build to win",
              "Build four chows, pungs or kongs and a pair, counting both declared and retained sets. A chow has three consecutive numbers in one suit, a pung three identical tiles, a kong four and a pair two. You also need 3 fan. When both conditions hold, choose Declare Mahjong and press Enter. The full scoring reference follows; drawing a matching tile does not end the hand automatically."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_claims, "Hong Kong \u2014 Claiming tiles and declaring kongs",
              "When another player discards, the response list offers only legal choices. Select Pass if you do not want the tile. To claim it, choose Chow, Pung, Kong or Declare Mahjong and press Enter. A chow may be claimed only from the player immediately before you. A pung or kong can use a discard from any opponent.",
              "A winning claim takes priority over a set claim; pung and kong take priority over chow. If several players can win on the same tile, the first in turn order after the discarder wins. The hand waits for every required response. After claiming chow or pung, discard without drawing; the claimed set stays exposed.",
              "After declaring a kong, draw a replacement tile before discarding. If you hold all four copies, the concealed-kong list lets you declare them together. You may also add your fourth tile to an exposed pung. Opponents can claim a win on that added tile before the kong is completed.",
              "Example: you hold two red dragons and someone discards a red dragon. Pung lets you take it and expose all three. You then discard one of your remaining tiles without first drawing. With bamboo 4\u20135, a bamboo 3 or 6 discarded by the player immediately before you may allow Chow. A matching tile from a different player cannot be claimed for Chow."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_sets, "Hong Kong \u2014 complete scoring reference",
              "A normal win needs four sets and a pair and at least 3 fan. Seven pairs and thirteen orphans use different shapes. The complete scoring reference for this version follows; entries allow open and closed hands unless restricted. An ordinary single chow, numbered pung or pair has no separate bonus.",
              "Compatible ordinary bonuses add together. Little and big three dragons replace the component dragon-pung bonuses. Special hands worth 10 fan or more use only the highest applicable special award, without ordinary bonuses. A concealed hand or four concealed pungs has no separate award. All eight bonus tiles give 4 fan; seven pairs give 4 fan."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_1, "Hong Kong: 1 fan",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Self-draw: draw the winning tile yourself.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 All chows: four sequences and a pair, which may be honours.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung or kong of red dragons.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung or kong of green dragons.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung or kong of white dragons.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung or kong of your seat wind.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung or kong of the round wind; matching seat and round wind scores 2 fan in total.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Win on a kong replacement tile; self-draw also counts.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Robbing a kong: win on the tile being added to an exposed pung.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Last ordinary wall draw; self-draw also counts.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Win on the last discard after the ordinary wall is empty.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 No bonus tiles: neither flowers nor seasons.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Your flower: its number matches your seat wind.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Your season: its number matches your seat wind."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_2, "Hong Kong: 2 fan",
              "- 2 fan \u2014 All four flowers: replaces the 1 fan for your flower.",
              "- 2 fan \u2014 All four seasons: replaces the 1 fan for your season."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_3_8, "Hong Kong: 3\u20138 fan",
              "- 3 fan \u2014 All pungs: four pungs or kongs and a pair.",
              "- 3 fan \u2014 Half flush: one numbered suit and honours, both present.",
              "- 4 fan \u2014 Seven pairs: four identical tiles not declared as a kong may count as two pairs.",
              "- 5 fan \u2014 Little three dragons: two dragon pungs or kongs and a pair of the third; includes dragon-pung bonuses.",
              "- 7 fan \u2014 Full flush: only Bamboo, only Dots or only Characters, no honours.",
              "- 8 fan \u2014 Big three dragons: pungs or kongs of all three dragons; includes dragon-pung bonuses."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_special, "Hong Kong: 10\u201318 fan",
              "- 10 fan \u2014 Little four winds: three wind pungs or kongs and a pair of the fourth.",
              "- 10 fan \u2014 All honours: a valid winning hand using only winds and dragons.",
              "- 10 fan \u2014 All terminals: only numbered 1s and 9s, no honours.",
              "- 13 fan \u2014 Thirteen orphans: every suited 1 and 9, all four winds and three dragons, plus one duplicate for a pair; closed hand.",
              "- 15 fan \u2014 Big four winds: four wind pungs or kongs and a pair.",
              "- 15 fan \u2014 Nine gates: concealed exact base 1112345678999 in one suit before the winning draw or claim of another tile of that suit.",
              "- 18 fan \u2014 Four kongs and a pair."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_bonus, "Hong Kong \u2014 flowers and seasons by seat wind",
              "Your flower and season follow your seat wind, not the round wind. Number 1 is East, 2 South, 3 West, 4 North. A complete family replaces its own-tile bonus; the two families score separately.",
              "- East: Plum flower and Spring.",
              "- South: Orchid flower and Summer.",
              "- West: Chrysanthemum flower and Autumn.",
              "- North: Bamboo flower and Winter."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_payments, "Hong Kong \u2014 Scoring and payments",
              "Payment bases are: 3 fan \u2014 8 points; 4 \u2014 16; 5 \u2014 24; 6 \u2014 32; 7 \u2014 48; 8 or more \u2014 64. A hand may exceed 8 fan, but its payment base does not increase.",
              "On a discard win, the discarder pays two bases and the other opponents one each. On self-draw, every opponent pays two bases.",
              "Heavenly and earthly hands are not currently awarded during play. Do not add a special opening-win bonus for them."),
            rule_section(:table_winds, "Hong Kong \u2014 Hands and match length",
              "The default match has four wind rounds: East, South, West and North. Every player completes a dealer period in each round, making at least sixteen hands. Hand payments accumulate from zero; winning one hand does not necessarily win the match.",
              "The dealer repeats after their win or any draw, so East takes at least four hands and can take more. Four wind rounds are East, South, West and North: at least 16 hands. Seasons are bonus tiles, not these rounds.",
              "After a hand, inspect the winner, hand value and point changes. In a longer match everyone chooses Ready for the next hand. A draw changes no hand points in Hong Kong. After the selected rounds, the highest total wins; equal highest totals tie."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_variants, "Hong Kong \u2014 Match length options",
              "Choose match length in the game options before play. The following shorter settings change how many hands the match contains, not the rules for completing a winning hand.",
              "One hand ends the game after one win or wall draw. East round means that all four players complete a dealer period. The dealer is East, but the shared round wind also remains East throughout that round. It is not one personal turn."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_aim, "Riichi \u2014 Aim and winning conditions",
              "In the standard four-player game you aim to complete your hand before the others. Usually it has four sets and a pair, but it also needs a yaku: a recognised winning condition. A complete shape without yaku may be unable to win. The match winner has the highest final points; the following terms explain these conditions."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_terms, "Riichi \u2014 How the terms work",
              "You play against the other players. Tiles in your hand are yours to arrange into a winning pattern; you do not remove matching pairs from a layout as in Solitaire. A hand is one deal and its play. A match may contain many hands.",
              "- A tile is one piece, such as bamboo 5. Its kind is its suit and number, or its particular wind or dragon. There are four copies of each ordinary kind.",
              "- Suits are characters, bamboo and circles, numbered 1\u20139. Different suits do not mix in one sequence. Terminals are 1 and 9; simples are 2\u20138.",
              "- Honors are East, South, West and North winds, and white, green and red dragons. They have no number and cannot form a sequence.",
              "- A chow or chi is three consecutive numbers in one suit: bamboo 3, 4 and 5. Bamboo 3, circles 4 and bamboo 5 do not qualify. Nor do 8, 9 and 1 of one suit.",
              "- A pung, also written pong or pon, is three identical tiles, such as three red dragons. Three fives from different suits are not a pung.",
              "- A kong or kan is four identical tiles. You must declare it through the kong action; simply holding four copies does not declare a kong. It is one set, with an extra replacement draw.",
              "- A pair is two identical tiles. A set is a chow, pung or kong. An ordinary winning hand needs four sets and one pair, not merely several pairs or a large number of tiles of one suit.",
              "- The wall is the supply of tiles you draw from. Drawing adds a tile to your hand; discarding removes one tile from your hand and offers it to opponents.",
              "- A closed hand has no sets claimed from opponents. Claiming a chow, pung or exposed kong opens it. Sets formed entirely from your own tiles can stay concealed. A declared concealed kong keeps your hand closed.",
              "- A self-draw win uses the tile you have just drawn. A discard win uses an opponent\u2019s discarded tile to finish your hand. Claim Mahjong to win; do not discard the winning tile first.",
              "- A wait is a tile kind that would finish your hand. With bamboo 4\u20135 you might wait for bamboo 3 or 6. Being one tile from a complete shape is called tenpai. A complete shape must still satisfy this variant\u2019s scoring conditions.",
              "- Yaku is a named condition that makes a winning shape eligible. You need at least one yaku. For example, a pung of dragons is a yaku; a random complete shape is not necessarily one.",
              "- Han measure the value of yaku and bonuses. Fu are smaller points for how your sets, pair and wait are built. Han and fu together determine the payment. Fu alone do not supply the required yaku.",
              "- Ron means a win on an opponent\u2019s discard; tsumo means a win on your own draw. Menzen means closed. Menzen tsumo is therefore a closed-hand self-draw win and is itself a yaku.",
              "- Riichi is a declaration that your closed hand is one tile from completion. It costs a 1000-point deposit and supplies a yaku, but restricts later changes to your hand.",
              "- Dora is a bonus tile kind named by an indicator. Each copy adds han after you have a real yaku. Holding dora alone does not permit a win.",
              "- Furiten is a restriction on winning from other players\u2019 discards. It can arise from your own discards or from declining a winning discard. It does not prevent self-draw wins.",
              "- Honba is the carry-over counter that adds payments after draws or dealer continuations. Mangan and higher limits are payment levels for valuable hands, not extra sets you must build.",
              "- Your seat wind is your role relative to the dealer: East for the dealer, then South, West and North. Round wind is shared by the table. A pung of either value wind can score, and one set can serve both roles when they coincide."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_start, "Riichi \u2014 Starting the hand",
              "Everyone starts with 30,000 points. Payments and accepted Riichi deposits change that balance.",
              "The dealer starts with 14 tiles and discards first. Everyone else starts with 13. On a normal turn, choose Draw a tile and press Enter. Drawing is an action; moving through the tile list does not draw."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_play, "Riichi \u2014 Your first turns",
              "Before your first discard, read your hand with Up and Down. Think of numbered tiles as possible sequences, identical tiles as possible pungs, and one pair as your eventual pair. You do not select all four sets in the interface: sets left in your hand are recognised when you declare a win.",
              "While you browse game fields, a new turn focuses Draw a tile. After drawing, focus moves to the newly drawn tile in your hand, marked just drawn. This does not discard it. You can move to another tile with the arrows or keep the new tile and discard an old one.",
              "To discard, find one tile in the available-to-discard list and press Enter. You lose that copy from your hand. If you hold several identical copies, discarding one does not remove the others.",
              "After another player discards, listen to the tile and read the response list. Pass means you do not claim that tile; it does not end your participation or concede the hand. If everyone passes, the next player draws. Passing several times does not mean you must draw each time: it may still be another player\u2019s turn.",
              "The first field describes the current situation. All available actions follow it: responses, Draw, Declare Mahjong, kong choices or Riichi when applicable. Your tile lists come after those actions. Tab and Shift+Tab move between fields; arrows choose within a list, and Enter confirms. An unavailable action is omitted.",
              "Read Exposed sets to inspect everyone\u2019s declared sets; each entry names its owner. Discards records which tiles each player has discarded. These fields help you see what is already used and what opponents may need. You can review them without submitting a move.",
              "If a tile completes an eligible winning hand, choose Declare Mahjong rather than discarding it or passing. The game checks both the shape and this variant\u2019s scoring requirements. If the wall runs out first, the hand is a draw; nobody wins merely for having the most nearly complete hand.",
              "Press S to hear the current points of every player. Press M to hear how many tiles remain in the wall for drawing. These are read-only announcements: they do not draw, discard or pass, and do not reveal other hands.",
              "W reads a legal winning hand value: fan or han and fu, and the points gained. Without a legal win it does not give a final hand value. Before a Riichi win it excludes hidden ura-dora, so the final value may be higher. U reads your exposed sets, Shift+U those of the other players, and R the hand number, winds and current situation. In Riichi, I reads dora indicators and tiles, your dora count, Riichi declarations, deposits, honba and your furiten restriction. These keys only read information."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_build, "Riichi \u2014 What you must build to win",
              "The ordinary shape is four sets and a pair. Seven distinct pairs and thirteen orphans are alternatives. A win requires yaku; dora and fu alone do not qualify. When a legal win is available, choose Declare Mahjong and press Enter. The references below give every awarded yaku and the fu values."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_claims, "Riichi \u2014 Claiming tiles and declaring kongs",
              "When another player discards, the response list offers only legal choices. Select Pass if you do not want the tile. To claim it, choose Chow, Pung, Kong or Declare Mahjong and press Enter. A chow may be claimed only from the player immediately before you. A pung or kong can use a discard from any opponent.",
              "A winning claim takes priority over a set claim; pung and kong take priority over chow. If several players can win on the same tile, the first in turn order after the discarder wins. The hand waits for every required response. After claiming chow or pung, discard without drawing; the claimed set stays exposed.",
              "After declaring a kong, draw a replacement tile before discarding. If you hold all four copies, the concealed-kong list lets you declare them together. You may also add your fourth tile to an exposed pung. Opponents can claim a win on that added tile before the kong is completed.",
              "Opening your hand can remove your planned yaku. Riichi and closed self-draw require a closed hand, while dragon pungs still score when exposed. Before claiming Chow or Pung, decide what yaku will remain."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi, "Riichi \u2014 declaring readiness, dora and furiten",
              "If your closed hand is one tile from winning, the Riichi list offers legal declaration discards. Select one and press Enter. An accepted declaration places 1,000 points on the table. Afterwards you must discard the drawn tile unless it wins; only a kong preserving all required hand interpretations is allowed. The winner collects the deposits; an unclaimed deposit carries to the next hand.",
              "Furiten prevents winning on a discard when one of your winning tiles appears among your own discards. Passing a legal winning discard also blocks discard wins temporarily; after declaring Riichi, this block lasts for the rest of the hand. You can still win by drawing the tile yourself.",
              "Suppose you wait for bamboo 3 or 6 but previously discarded bamboo 3. Furiten blocks a discard win on both winning kinds, including 6; it is not limited to the exact tile you discarded. A self-drawn 3 or 6 can still win if the hand qualifies."),
            rule_section(:table_yaku, "Riichi \u2014 complete yaku and han reference",
              "Riichi needs a valid hand and at least one yaku. Below are all ordinary yaku awarded in this version, with closed and open values. A concealed kong does not open the hand. A pung completed by the winning discard is not concealed when scoring that win.",
              "- Riichi: a closed ready hand and accepted declaration with a 1000-point deposit. Closed only: 1 han.",
              "- Double riichi: declaration in the first uninterrupted circuit; replaces ordinary riichi. Closed only: 2 han.",
              "- Ippatsu: win in the first uninterrupted circuit after riichi. Closed only: 1 han.",
              "- Menzen tsumo: closed-hand self-draw. Closed only: 1 han.",
              "- Tanyao: only numbered 2 to 8, without honours. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Yakuhai: each dragon pung or kong separately. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Seat wind pung or kong. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Round wind pung or kong; matching seat wind gives 2 han in total. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Haitei: win on the last ordinary wall draw. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Houtei: win on the last discard. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Rinshan: win on the replacement draw after a kong. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Chankan: robbing a kong. Closed: 1 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Pinfu: four chows, a non-value pair and a two-sided wait. Closed only: 1 han.",
              "- Iipeikou: two identical chows in one suit. Closed only: 1 han.",
              "- Ryanpeikou: two pairs of identical chows; replaces iipeikou. Closed only: 3 han.",
              "- Chiitoitsu: seven distinct pairs; four identical tiles cannot count as two pairs. Closed only: 2 han.",
              "- Toitoi: four pungs or kongs and a pair. Closed: 2 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Sanankou: three concealed pungs or kongs; the fourth set may be open. Closed: 2 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Sankantsu: three kongs. Closed: 2 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Shousangen: two dragon pungs or kongs and a pair of the third; both dragon yakuhai also count. Closed: 2 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Sanshoku doujun: the same chow in all three suits. Closed: 2 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Sanshoku doukou: pungs of the same number in all three suits. Closed: 2 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Ittsu: chows 123, 456 and 789 in one suit. Closed: 2 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Chanta: every set and pair contains a terminal or honour, with both chows and honours present. Closed: 2 han; open: 1 han.",
              "- Junchan: every set and pair contains a 1 or 9, with chows and no honours. Closed: 3 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Honroutou: only terminals and honours. Closed: 2 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Honitsu: one numbered suit and honours. Closed: 3 han; open: 2 han.",
              "- Chinitsu: one numbered suit without honours. Closed: 6 han; open: 5 han."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_dora, "Riichi \u2014 bonus han from dora",
              "Dora add 1 han for each owned dora tile, but only to a hand with a real yaku. Dora are not yaku. An indicator points to the next tile in its cycle; every copy in the hand or own sets counts. After accepted riichi, hidden ura-dora may add to the final win; the pre-win preview does not reveal them."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_yakuman, "Riichi \u2014 complete yakuman reference",
              "Each entry below awards 1 yakuman and replaces ordinary han and fu. Different compatible yakuman can stack. This version does not double a yakuman for a special wait or for big four winds.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Thirteen orphans: all terminals and honours plus one duplicate; closed hand.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Four concealed pungs or kongs: a discard win must complete the pair.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Big three dragons: pungs or kongs of all three dragons.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Big four winds: four wind pungs or kongs.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Little four winds: three wind pungs or kongs and a pair of the fourth.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 All honours: only winds and dragons.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 All terminals: only 1s and 9s.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 All green: only Bamboo 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 and green dragons.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Four kongs and a pair.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Nine gates: closed single-suit hand, 1112345678999 plus one extra tile of that suit.",
              "Heavenly and earthly hands are not currently awarded during play. Do not add a special opening-win bonus for them."),
            rule_section(:table_fu, "Riichi: fu for sets and waits",
              "Han measure yaku and bonuses; fu measure features of the hand\u2019s construction. A chow gives 0 fu. An ordinary pair also gives 0; a dragon, seat-wind or round-wind pair gives 2. This game still awards 2 fu when the same wind is both your seat and the round wind.",
              "Pung and kong values depend on exposure and tile type:",
              "- Pung of simples, numbers 2\u20138: exposed 2 fu, concealed 4 fu.",
              "- Pung of terminals or honors: exposed 4 fu, concealed 8 fu.",
              "- Kong of simples: exposed 8 fu, concealed 16 fu.",
              "- Kong of terminals or honors: exposed 16 fu, concealed 32 fu.",
              "A pung completed by the winning discard counts as exposed for fu. Other pungs held in your hand stay concealed. Adding a fourth tile to an exposed pung makes an exposed kong.",
              "Ordinary calculation starts at 20 fu. A closed discard win adds 10; self-draw adds 2. A pair wait, closed wait or edge wait adds 2. Edge means waiting for 3 with 1\u20132, or 7 with 8\u20139; a closed wait means, for example, 5 with 4\u20136. A two-sided wait such as 3 or 6 with 4\u20135 adds no fu.",
              "Round the total up to a multiple of ten: 32 becomes 40. Seven pairs is always 25 fu. Pinfu self-draw is 20 fu without the extra 2 for self-draw. Any other hand that would have only 20 receives a minimum of 30.",
              "For example, 20 base, 10 for a closed discard win, 4 for a concealed pung of fives and 2 for a dragon pair total 36, rounded to 40 fu. You still need a yaku: fu alone cannot make the hand eligible to win."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_payments, "Riichi: converting han and fu to points",
              "For an ordinary win the base is fu multiplied by 2 to the power of han plus 2, capped at 2000. With 1 han and 30 fu it is 30 times 8, or 240. Round each final payment up to hundreds.",
              "On a discard, a non-dealer winner receives four bases from the discarder; a dealer receives six. On self-draw, a non-dealer receives two bases from the dealer and one from each other player. When the dealer wins, everyone pays two bases.",
              "For 1 han 30 fu without bonuses, a non-dealer wins 1000 on a discard, or 500 from the dealer and 300 from each other opponent on self-draw, totaling 1100. A dealer wins 1500 on a discard or 500 from each opponent, also 1500.",
              "- Mangan: 5 han or reaching the base cap; this game also includes 3 han 60 fu and 4 han 30 fu. Discard payments are 8000 for a non-dealer or 12,000 for a dealer.",
              "- Haneman: 6\u20137 han, respectively 12,000 or 18,000 on a discard.",
              "- Baiman: 8\u201310 han, respectively 16,000 or 24,000.",
              "- Sanbaiman: 11\u201312 han, respectively 24,000 or 36,000.",
              "- Yakuman or at least 13 han: respectively 32,000 or 48,000. Multiple compatible yakuman multiply that payment.",
              "Each honba adds 300 to a discard payment or 100 from each opponent on self-draw. Riichi deposits are awarded to the winner in addition. Honba increases after a draw or dealer repeat and resets after another player wins."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_match, "Riichi \u2014 Hands and match length",
              "The default is four players and East and South rounds, with 30,000 starting points each. Everyone completes a dealer period in East, then in South: at least eight hands, with dealer continuations adding more. Payments and accepted Riichi deposits change the balances during the match.",
              "The dealer repeats after a dealer win or an exhaustive draw with a tenpai dealer.",
              "On a wall draw, 3000 points move from noten to tenpai hands, unless everyone or nobody is tenpai. After each hand everyone confirms readiness. The selected rounds decide the end; this version does not stop early for a negative score or a leading final dealer. Highest final points wins, with tied highest scores a draw."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_variants, "Riichi \u2014 three-player variant and match length",
              "Choose match length in the game options before play. The following shorter settings change how many hands the match contains, not the rules for completing a winning hand.",
              "One hand ends after one win or wall draw. East round gives each player a dealer period: at least four hands with four players or three in sanma. East and South gives two such rounds: at least eight or six hands. The dealer repeats after winning, or after a draw when tenpai, so the actual count may be larger.",
              "The seat wind follows the current dealer: East, South, West, then North with four players. The round wind stays East until everyone has completed a dealer period, then becomes South in a two-round match. Value-wind sets may therefore score differently later. Sanma removes characters 2\u20138 and does not provide a separate North extraction action in this version.",
              "In sanma you cannot claim Chow, but you may form sequences from your own numbered tiles.",
              "In sanma the absent fourth player does not pay, and that share is not redistributed. Self-draw can therefore earn less than with four players. Draw settlements divide 2000 between tenpai and noten players, instead of 3000 with four players."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_aim, "MCR \u2014 Aim and winning conditions",
              "Four players try to finish a winning hand first. The usual shape is four sets and a pair, with at least 8 fan before flower bonuses. Fan are the value of compatible scoring patterns. A hand win earns a payment; the match winner is the player with the highest combined balance."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_terms, "MCR \u2014 How the terms work",
              "You play against the other players. Tiles in your hand are yours to arrange into a winning pattern; you do not remove matching pairs from a layout as in Solitaire. A hand is one deal and its play. A match may contain many hands.",
              "- A tile is one piece, such as bamboo 5. Its kind is its suit and number, or its particular wind or dragon. There are four copies of each ordinary kind.",
              "- Suits are characters, bamboo and circles, numbered 1\u20139. Different suits do not mix in one sequence. Terminals are 1 and 9; simples are 2\u20138.",
              "- Honors are East, South, West and North winds, and white, green and red dragons. They have no number and cannot form a sequence.",
              "- A chow or chi is three consecutive numbers in one suit: bamboo 3, 4 and 5. Bamboo 3, circles 4 and bamboo 5 do not qualify. Nor do 8, 9 and 1 of one suit.",
              "- A pung, also written pong or pon, is three identical tiles, such as three red dragons. Three fives from different suits are not a pung.",
              "- A kong or kan is four identical tiles. You must declare it through the kong action; simply holding four copies does not declare a kong. It is one set, with an extra replacement draw.",
              "- A pair is two identical tiles. A set is a chow, pung or kong. An ordinary winning hand needs four sets and one pair, not merely several pairs or a large number of tiles of one suit.",
              "- The wall is the supply of tiles you draw from. Drawing adds a tile to your hand; discarding removes one tile from your hand and offers it to opponents.",
              "- A closed hand has no sets claimed from opponents. Claiming a chow, pung or exposed kong opens it. Sets formed entirely from your own tiles can stay concealed. A declared concealed kong keeps your hand closed.",
              "- A self-draw win uses the tile you have just drawn. A discard win uses an opponent\u2019s discarded tile to finish your hand. Claim Mahjong to win; do not discard the winning tile first.",
              "- A wait is a tile kind that would finish your hand. With bamboo 4\u20135 you might wait for bamboo 3 or 6. Being one tile from a complete shape is called tenpai. A complete shape must still satisfy this variant\u2019s scoring conditions.",
              "- MCR means Mahjong Competition Rules. Fan measure scoring patterns. You need at least 8 fan before flower bonuses. Fan are then used in the payment calculation; they are not the same as your total match score.",
              "- A scoring pattern describes a feature of the whole hand or its sets. Some patterns can combine; others already include a smaller pattern and exclude its extra points. Do not sum every name that seems to fit.",
              "- Flowers and seasons are exposed and replaced automatically. Each adds 1 fan to a valid win but does not help reach the required 8. A 7-fan hand plus a flower cannot win.",
              "- Seat wind is your role relative to the dealer; round wind is shared by everyone. A pung of your seat wind or round wind is a scoring pattern. Seasons are bonus tiles, unrelated to the four wind rounds."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_start, "MCR \u2014 Starting the hand",
              "Balances start at zero; winning hands add payments and losing them subtracts payments.",
              "The dealer starts with 14 tiles and discards first. Everyone else starts with 13. On a normal turn, choose Draw a tile and press Enter. Drawing is an action; moving through the tile list does not draw."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_play, "MCR \u2014 Your first turns",
              "Before your first discard, read your hand with Up and Down. Think of numbered tiles as possible sequences, identical tiles as possible pungs, and one pair as your eventual pair. You do not select all four sets in the interface: sets left in your hand are recognised when you declare a win.",
              "While you browse game fields, a new turn focuses Draw a tile. After drawing, focus moves to the newly drawn tile in your hand, marked just drawn. This does not discard it. You can move to another tile with the arrows or keep the new tile and discard an old one.",
              "To discard, find one tile in the available-to-discard list and press Enter. You lose that copy from your hand. If you hold several identical copies, discarding one does not remove the others.",
              "After another player discards, listen to the tile and read the response list. Pass means you do not claim that tile; it does not end your participation or concede the hand. If everyone passes, the next player draws. Passing several times does not mean you must draw each time: it may still be another player\u2019s turn.",
              "The first field describes the current situation. All available actions follow it: responses, Draw, Declare Mahjong, kong choices or Riichi when applicable. Your tile lists come after those actions. Tab and Shift+Tab move between fields; arrows choose within a list, and Enter confirms. An unavailable action is omitted.",
              "Read Exposed sets to inspect everyone\u2019s declared sets; each entry names its owner. Discards records which tiles each player has discarded. These fields help you see what is already used and what opponents may need. You can review them without submitting a move.",
              "If a tile completes an eligible winning hand, choose Declare Mahjong rather than discarding it or passing. The game checks both the shape and this variant\u2019s scoring requirements. If the wall runs out first, the hand is a draw; nobody wins merely for having the most nearly complete hand.",
              "Press S to hear the current points of every player. Press M to hear how many tiles remain in the wall for drawing. These are read-only announcements: they do not draw, discard or pass, and do not reveal other hands.",
              "W reads a legal winning hand value: fan or han and fu, and the points gained. Without a legal win it does not give a final hand value. Before a Riichi win it excludes hidden ura-dora, so the final value may be higher. U reads your exposed sets, Shift+U those of the other players, and R the hand number, winds and current situation. In Riichi, I reads dora indicators and tiles, your dora count, Riichi declarations, deposits, honba and your furiten restriction. These keys only read information."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_build, "MCR \u2014 What you must build to win",
              "The normal shape is four sets and a pair; MCR also allows seven pairs, thirteen orphans and the knitted hands defined in the reference. You need at least 8 fan without flowers. When eligible, choose Declare Mahjong and press Enter. Each catalog entry gives its name, value and exposure requirement."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_claims, "MCR \u2014 Claiming tiles and declaring kongs",
              "When another player discards, the response list offers only legal choices. Select Pass if you do not want the tile. To claim it, choose Chow, Pung, Kong or Declare Mahjong and press Enter. A chow may be claimed only from the player immediately before you. A pung or kong can use a discard from any opponent.",
              "A winning claim takes priority over a set claim; pung and kong take priority over chow. If several players can win on the same tile, the first in turn order after the discarder wins. The hand waits for every required response. After claiming chow or pung, discard without drawing; the claimed set stays exposed.",
              "After declaring a kong, draw a replacement tile before discarding. If you hold all four copies, the concealed-kong list lets you declare them together. You may also add your fourth tile to an exposed pung. Opponents can claim a win on that added tile before the kong is completed.",
              "Suppose you have two white dragons and a third is discarded. Pung exposes them and secures a 2-fan dragon pattern, but you still need the rest of your hand to reach 8. Claiming a tile changes which concealed-hand patterns remain available. A legal Pung button does not promise that your eventual hand will meet the winning minimum."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_sets, "MCR \u2014 using the 81-entry reference",
              "The following 81 entries describe MCR scoring elements. A valid hand shape and at least 8 fan excluding flowers are required. A kong also qualifies wherever a pung of that kind is required. The three numbered suits are Characters, Dots and Bamboo; honours are winds and dragons.",
              "A closed hand has no sets claimed from discards; a declared concealed kong does not open it. A pung completed by the winning discard counts as exposed; other retained pungs remain concealed. A knitted set uses 147 in one suit, 258 in another and 369 in the third, in any suit assignment.",
              "Do not add every matching name without checking exclusions. A larger pattern can include a smaller one, and the same sets cannot be freely reused for compound bonuses. The game selects compatible patterns. Numbers in the exclusion list refer to MCR catalog entries, not a sequence of moves."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_88, "MCR: 88 fan",
              "- MCR 1: 88 fan \u2014 Big four winds: four wind pungs. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 2: 88 fan \u2014 Big three dragons: three dragon pungs. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 3: 88 fan \u2014 All green: only Bamboo 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 and green dragons. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 4: 88 fan \u2014 Nine gates: concealed 1112345678999 in one suit, completed with another tile of that suit. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 5: 88 fan \u2014 Four kongs. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 6: 88 fan \u2014 Seven consecutive pairs in one suit. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 7: 88 fan \u2014 Thirteen orphans: all different terminals and honours, plus one duplicate forming a pair. Closed hand only."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_64, "MCR: 64 fan",
              "- MCR 8: 64 fan \u2014 All terminals: only numbered 1s and 9s. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 9: 64 fan \u2014 Little four winds: three wind pungs and a pair of the fourth wind. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 10: 64 fan \u2014 Little three dragons: two dragon pungs and a pair of the third dragon. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 11: 64 fan \u2014 All honours: only winds and dragons. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 12: 64 fan \u2014 Four concealed pungs. All four pungs concealed.",
              "- MCR 13: 64 fan \u2014 Pure terminal chows: 123, 123, 789, 789 and pair 55, all in one suit. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_48, "MCR: 48 fan",
              "- MCR 14: 48 fan \u2014 Four identical chows in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 15: 48 fan \u2014 Four consecutive numbered pungs in one suit. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_32, "MCR: 32 fan",
              "- MCR 16: 32 fan \u2014 Four shifted chows in one suit: every shift is 1, or every shift is 2. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 17: 32 fan \u2014 Three kongs. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 18: 32 fan \u2014 All terminals and honours, with both types present. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_24, "MCR: 24 fan",
              "- MCR 19: 24 fan \u2014 Seven pairs. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 20: 24 fan \u2014 Greater honours and knitted tiles: all seven honours and seven different tiles from a knitted set. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 21: 24 fan \u2014 All even pungs: pungs or kongs and a pair, using only 2, 4, 6 and 8. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 22: 24 fan \u2014 Full flush: one numbered suit without honours. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 23: 24 fan \u2014 Three identical chows in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 24: 24 fan \u2014 Three consecutive numbered pungs in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 25: 24 fan \u2014 Only numbers 7 to 9. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 26: 24 fan \u2014 Only numbers 4 to 6. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 27: 24 fan \u2014 Only numbers 1 to 3. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_16, "MCR: 16 fan",
              "- MCR 28: 16 fan \u2014 Pure straight: 123, 456 and 789 in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 29: 16 fan \u2014 Three-suited terminal chows: 123 and 789 in each of two suits, pair 55 in the third. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 30: 16 fan \u2014 Three shifted chows in one suit, with equal steps of 1 or 2. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 31: 16 fan \u2014 Every set and the pair contain a numbered 5. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 32: 16 fan \u2014 Three pungs of the same number, one in each suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 33: 16 fan \u2014 Three concealed pungs. These three pungs concealed; the fourth set may be exposed."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_12, "MCR: 12 fan",
              "- MCR 34: 12 fan \u2014 Lesser honours and knitted tiles: 14 different tiles chosen from all seven honours and a nine-tile knitted set. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 35: 12 fan \u2014 Knitted straight: 147, 258 and 369, each group in a different suit, plus a set and a pair. The nine knitted tiles concealed; the additional set may be exposed.",
              "- MCR 36: 12 fan \u2014 Only numbers 6 to 9. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 37: 12 fan \u2014 Only numbers 1 to 4. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 38: 12 fan \u2014 Three pungs of different winds. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_8, "MCR: 8 fan",
              "- MCR 39: 8 fan \u2014 Mixed straight: 123, 456 and 789, each chow in a different suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 40: 8 fan \u2014 Reversible tiles: Dots 1 to 5, 8, 9; Bamboo 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9; white dragon. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 41: 8 fan \u2014 Three identical chows, one in each suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 42: 8 fan \u2014 Three consecutive numbered pungs, each in a different suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 43: 8 fan \u2014 Chicken hand: no other scoring element, disregarding flowers; open hand won on a discard. Open hand; win on a discard.",
              "- MCR 44: 8 fan \u2014 Win on the last ordinary wall draw. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 45: 8 fan \u2014 Win on the discard following the last ordinary wall draw. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 46: 8 fan \u2014 Win on a replacement tile drawn after a kong, not a flower replacement. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 47: 8 fan \u2014 Robbing a kong: win on the tile added to an exposed pung. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 48: 8 fan \u2014 Two concealed kongs. Both kongs concealed; other sets may be exposed."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_6, "MCR: 6 fan",
              "- MCR 49: 6 fan \u2014 All pungs: four pungs or kongs and a pair. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 50: 6 fan \u2014 Half flush: one numbered suit and honours, both present. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 51: 6 fan \u2014 Three chows shifted by 1 each time, each in a different suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 52: 6 fan \u2014 All types: all three suits, at least one wind and one dragon. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 53: 6 fan \u2014 Melded hand: all four sets claimed from discards, pair completed on a discard; no concealed kong. Open hand; win on a discard.",
              "- MCR 54: 6 fan \u2014 Two dragon pungs. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_4, "MCR: 4 fan",
              "- MCR 55: 4 fan \u2014 Outside hand: every set and the pair contain a terminal or honour. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 56: 4 fan \u2014 Fully concealed hand won by self-draw. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 57: 4 fan \u2014 Two exposed kongs. Both kongs exposed.",
              "- MCR 58: 4 fan \u2014 Last of its kind: the other three identical tiles are already publicly visible. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_2, "MCR: 2 fan",
              "- MCR 59: 2 fan \u2014 Dragon pung. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 60: 2 fan \u2014 Round wind pung. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 61: 2 fan \u2014 Seat wind pung. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 62: 2 fan \u2014 Concealed hand won on a discard; concealed kongs are allowed. Closed hand only.",
              "- MCR 63: 2 fan \u2014 All chows: four chows and a numbered pair, without honours. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 64: 2 fan \u2014 Four identical tiles used outside a declared kong. Open or closed hand. For each tile kind separately.",
              "- MCR 65: 2 fan \u2014 Two pungs of the same number in different suits. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 66: 2 fan \u2014 Two concealed pungs. These two pungs concealed; other sets may be exposed.",
              "- MCR 67: 2 fan \u2014 One concealed kong. This kong concealed; other sets may be exposed.",
              "- MCR 68: 2 fan \u2014 All simples: only numbers 2 to 8, without honours. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_1, "MCR: 1 fan",
              "- MCR 69: 1 fan \u2014 Two identical chows in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 70: 1 fan \u2014 Two matching chows in different suits. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 71: 1 fan \u2014 Short straight: two chows making six consecutive numbers in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 72: 1 fan \u2014 Two terminal chows: 123 and 789 in one suit. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 73: 1 fan \u2014 Pung of 1s, 9s or a wind that is neither seat nor round wind. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 74: 1 fan \u2014 One exposed kong. This kong exposed.",
              "- MCR 75: 1 fan \u2014 One voided suit: exactly two numbered suits are present and the third is absent; honours are allowed. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 76: 1 fan \u2014 No honours. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 77: 1 fan \u2014 Edge wait: 12 waiting for 3, or 89 waiting for 7; the only winning tile kind. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 78: 1 fan \u2014 Closed wait: a middle tile, such as 24 waiting for 3; the only winning tile kind. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 79: 1 fan \u2014 Pair wait: the only winning tile kind completes the pair. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 80: 1 fan \u2014 Self-draw: draw the winning tile yourself. Open or closed hand.",
              "- MCR 81: 1 fan \u2014 Each flower or season: one extra point, excluded from the eight-fan minimum. Open or closed hand."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_mixed_kongs, "MCR \u2014 two mixed kongs: 6 fan",
              "Two mixed kongs \u2014 6 fan: one exposed and one concealed kong. This replaces the individual kong bonuses. This version distinguishes that combination in addition to the standard 81 named entries."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_exclusions, "MCR \u2014 bonuses included in larger patterns",
              "Do not add every matching name without checking exclusions. A larger pattern can include a smaller one, and the same sets cannot be freely reused for compound bonuses. The game selects compatible patterns. Numbers in the exclusion list refer to MCR catalog entries, not a sequence of moves.",
              "- Do not add to entry 1: 9, 38, 49, 60, 61, 73.",
              "- Do not add to entry 2: 10, 54, 59.",
              "- Do not add to entry 4: 22, 62, 73, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 5: 17, 48, 49, 57, 67, 74, 79.",
              "- Do not add to entry 6: 19, 22, 62, 76, 79.",
              "- Do not add to entry 7: 18, 52, 62, 79.",
              "- Do not add to entry 8: 18, 49, 55, 73, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 9: 38, 73.",
              "- Do not add to entry 10: 54, 59.",
              "- Do not add to entry 11: 18, 49, 55, 73.",
              "- Do not add to entry 12: 33, 49, 62, 66.",
              "- Do not add to entry 13: 19, 22, 63, 69, 72, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 14: 23, 64, 69.",
              "- Do not add to entry 15: 24, 49.",
              "- Do not add to entry 16: 30, 71.",
              "- Do not add to entry 17: 48, 57, 67, 74.",
              "- Do not add to entry 18: 49, 55, 73.",
              "- Do not add to entry 19: 62, 79.",
              "- Do not add to entry 20: 34, 52, 62.",
              "- Do not add to entry 21: 49, 68, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 22: 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 23: 69.",
              "- Do not add to entry 25: 36, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 26: 68, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 27: 37, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 29: 63, 69, 70, 72, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 31: 68, 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 33: 66.",
              "- Do not add to entry 34: 52, 62.",
              "- Do not add to entry 36: 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 37: 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 40: 75.",
              "- Do not add to entry 44: 80.",
              "- Do not add to entry 46: 80.",
              "- Do not add to entry 47: 58.",
              "- Do not add to entry 48: 57, 66, 67, 74.",
              "- Do not add to entry 53: 62, 79.",
              "- Do not add to entry 54: 59.",
              "- Do not add to entry 56: 62, 80.",
              "- Do not add to entry 57: 67, 74.",
              "- Do not add to entry 63: 76.",
              "- Do not add to entry 68: 76.",
              "Three wind pungs (38) exclude the non-value wind pung point (73) only for those same pungs. Other independent pungs may still score."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_payments, "MCR \u2014 Scoring and payments",
              "Every opponent pays a base 8 points. On a discard win the discarder additionally pays the hand value including flowers; on self-draw every opponent adds that value. Each flower and season adds 1 fan to payment, but not to the 8-fan qualifying minimum."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_match, "MCR \u2014 Hands and match length",
              "The default match has four wind rounds and exactly sixteen hands, with balances starting at zero. East, South, West and North each last four hands. The dealer always changes after a hand, so a dealer win or a draw does not add an extra repeat.",
              "Seat wind changes with the dealer, while round wind changes only after the four dealer periods. This changes which wind pungs score. Seasons remain separate bonus tiles. After each hand inspect the result and score; everyone must confirm readiness before the next hand.",
              "A draw gives no hand points in MCR. After all sixteen hands the highest balance wins; equal highest balances tie. Winning many small hands and losing one very expensive hand can still leave you behind, so a hand win and a match win are different goals."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_variants, "MCR \u2014 Match length options",
              "Choose match length in the game options before play. The following shorter settings change how many hands the match contains, not the rules for completing a winning hand.",
              "One hand ends after one win or wall draw. Four wind rounds means sixteen hands: four with round wind East, four South, four West, four North. Within each round every player deals once. The dealer always advances, even after winning or a draw, so these sixteen hands do not grow through dealer repeats."),
            rule_section(:controls, "Keyboard reference",
              "Tab: next field.",
              "Shift+Tab: previous field.",
              "Up/Down: choose a tile or action in a list.",
              "Enter: confirm the selected action or discard.",
              "S: read every player\u2019s current points.",
              "M: read remaining wall tiles; in Solitaire, remaining layout tiles.",
              "Left/Right (Solitaire): move between columns.",
              "Up/Down (Solitaire): move between rows.",
              "1 (Solitaire): bottom layer.",
              "2 (Solitaire): second layer in Terraces.",
              "3 (Solitaire): top layer in Terraces.",
              "D (Solitaire): read layer, row and column.",
              "Enter (Solitaire): mark the first tile or remove a matching second tile.",
              "Escape (Solitaire): cancel the marked tile.",
              "D (table games): read the tile awaiting your response.",
              "W (table games): legal winning hand value and points gained.",
              "U (table games): your exposed sets.",
              "Shift+U (table games): other players exposed sets.",
              "R (table games): hand number, winds and current situation.",
              "I (Riichi): dora, declarations, deposits, honba and your furiten.")
          ]
        when "es"
          [
            rule_section(:table_aim, "Mahjong: objetivo",
              "En el Solitario predeterminado retiras parejas y ganas vaciando el dise\u00F1o. Hong Kong, Riichi y MCR son juegos distintos contra otras personas: formas una mano ganadora y consigues puntos. Cada uno tiene su parte completa, empezando por objetivo y t\u00E9rminos.",
              "Los sonidos distinguen el reparto, el robo y el descarte de una ficha, la declaraci\u00F3n de un grupo y el final de una mano. En Solitario tambi\u00E9n se oyen la retirada de una pareja y deshacer. Pasar y recorrer las fichas no producen sonidos. La descripci\u00F3n hablada sigue indicando la ficha y el resultado.",
              "Al responder a una ficha, D lee la ficha a la que se refiere Pasar, Chow, Pung, Kong o Mahjong. No cambia ni confirma la elecci\u00F3n. En Solitario, D sigue leyendo las coordenadas.",
              "W lee brevemente el total actual de bonificaciones reconocidas y sus motivos. Si dependen de conjuntos ocultos, indica que debes conservarlos ocultos. En MCR, dos, tres y cuatro pungs ocultos valen 2, 16 y 64 fan; estas bonificaciones se sustituyen, no se suman. No es el valor completo de una mano incompleta. Las flores de MCR y las dora de Riichi se anuncian aparte. Cuando es posible declarar Mahjong, W da el valor completo de la victoria. La opci\u00F3n de vista previa bajo W est\u00E1 activada por defecto; No conserva solo el valor de una victoria legal. No cambia la puntuaci\u00F3n.",
              "Tras una victoria, Valor de la mano enumera las bonificaciones realmente concedidas, sin patrones sustituidos. En MCR separa los fan de la mano de los extras por flores y estaciones e indica el total para el pago. Los extras no cuentan para el m\u00EDnimo de 8 fan."),
            rule_section(:aim, "Solitario \u2014 t\u00E9rminos y objetivo",
              "Retira las 144 fichas por parejas coincidentes. Juegas en solitario y ganas al retirar la \u00FAltima pareja.",
              "En el solitario, el dise\u00F1o es toda la disposici\u00F3n de fichas. Una pareja son dos piezas que coinciden; una capa es un nivel de altura; las filas van horizontalmente y las columnas verticalmente. Una ficha libre no tiene otra encima y tiene abierto al menos un lado, izquierdo o derecho. Puede estar visible y bloqueada por ambos lados.",
              "Aqu\u00ED no formas chows, pungs ni kongs ni cuentas fan, han o yaku. Esos t\u00E9rminos pertenecen a los juegos contra otras personas. En esta variante ganas retirando todas las parejas del dise\u00F1o."),
            rule_section(:tiles, "Solitario: Fichas coincidentes y libres",
              "Una pareja re\u00FAne dos fichas del mismo palo y n\u00FAmero, dos vientos iguales o dos dragones iguales. Dos flores cualesquiera coinciden entre s\u00ED, al igual que dos estaciones cualesquiera. Una flor no forma pareja con una estaci\u00F3n.",
              "Las dos fichas deben estar libres: ninguna ficha puede cubrirlas desde arriba y cada una debe tener libre su lado izquierdo o derecho. Una ficha visible puede seguir bloqueada por ambos lados. Las fichas cubiertas se anuncian sin revelar su valor."),
            rule_section(:play, "Solitario: Elegir una pareja",
              "En Fichas del dise\u00F1o, Izquierda y Derecha recorren las columnas; Arriba y Abajo recorren las filas. Pulsa 1, 2 o 3 para cambiar de capa en Terrazas; el dise\u00F1o plano solo tiene la capa 1. Una capa indica altura, no una fila. Pulsa D para o\u00EDr capa, fila y columna, numeradas por separado en cada capa.",
              "Cada ficha visible indica si est\u00E1 libre por la izquierda, por la derecha, por ambos lados o bloqueada por ambos lados. Una ficha cubierta no revela su identidad. Las casillas vac\u00EDas permanecen tras retirar fichas, de modo que las coordenadas de las dem\u00E1s no cambian.",
              "Busca una ficha libre y pulsa Enter para marcarla. Despu\u00E9s busca otra ficha libre que forme pareja y pulsa Enter para retirar ambas. La primera selecci\u00F3n no retira nada. Si la segunda no coincide, la primera sigue marcada. Pulsa Enter otra vez sobre la primera, o Escape, para cancelar. El juego no te muestra una lista de parejas resueltas.",
              "Retirar una pareja puede liberar fichas debajo o al lado. Piensa qu\u00E9 descubrir\u00E1 tu elecci\u00F3n. Un dise\u00F1o nuevo tiene soluci\u00F3n, pero algunas decisiones pueden dejarte sin otra pareja disponible.",
              "Ejemplo: busca un bamb\u00FA 5 libre y pulsa Enter para marcarlo. Busca otro bamb\u00FA 5 libre, quiz\u00E1 en otra capa, y pulsa Enter. Ambos desaparecen. Si el segundo est\u00E1 cubierto o bloqueado, lib\u00E9ralo antes retirando otras parejas.",
              "Si hay varias fichas id\u00E9nticas, piensa qu\u00E9 copias retirar. Una ficha del borde puede liberar a la vecina; una superior descubre la inferior. No toda pareja disponible conduce a la soluci\u00F3n. Deshacer la \u00FAltima pareja permite probar otro orden."),
            rule_section(:ending, "Solitario: Deshacer y terminar",
              "El bot\u00F3n para deshacer la \u00FAltima pareja devuelve las dos fichas retiradas m\u00E1s recientemente. Puedes usarlo varias veces para retroceder hasta la disposici\u00F3n inicial.",
              "El progreso indica cu\u00E1ntas parejas has retirado y cu\u00E1ntas fichas quedan. Tu puntuaci\u00F3n es el n\u00FAmero de parejas retiradas, hasta 72. Si no hay parejas disponibles, deshaz una jugada para probar otro camino o usa el bot\u00F3n para terminar este tablero. Terminar antes de retirar todas las fichas no cuenta como victoria.",
              "S lee la puntuaci\u00F3n, las parejas retiradas. M las fichas que quedan. Un dise\u00F1o nuevo tiene 144; al retirar una pareja quedan 142."),
            rule_section(:solitaire_points, "Solitario \u2014 puntos por retirar parejas",
              "El solitario no usa fan, han ni fu. La puntuaci\u00F3n es el n\u00FAmero de parejas retiradas: cada una da 1 punto y deshacerla lo resta. Ganas al retirar las 144 fichas, es decir, 72 parejas."),
            rule_section(:layout, "Solitario: Elegir la disposici\u00F3n",
              "Terrazas es la disposici\u00F3n predeterminada y tiene varias capas. La disposici\u00F3n plana utiliza una sola capa. Las reglas para formar parejas y dejar libre un lado son las mismas en ambas."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_aim, "Hong Kong \u2014 Objetivo y condiciones de victoria",
              "Cuatro jugadores buscan completar primero una mano ganadora. La forma normal son cuatro grupos y pareja por al menos 3 fan, unidades de valor de la mano. Gana la partida quien tenga m\u00E1s puntos totales. Los t\u00E9rminos siguientes explican grupos y unidades antes del primer turno."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_terms, "Hong Kong \u2014 Significado de los t\u00E9rminos",
              "Juegas contra los dem\u00E1s. Formas una mano ganadora con tus fichas; no retiras parejas de un dise\u00F1o como en el solitario. Tu mano es el conjunto de tus fichas. Una mano de juego va desde el reparto hasta una victoria o empate; la partida puede contener varias.",
              "- Una ficha es una pieza, como bamb\u00FA 5. Su tipo es el palo y n\u00FAmero, o el viento o drag\u00F3n concreto. Hay cuatro copias de cada tipo normal.",
              "- Los palos son caracteres, bamb\u00FAes y c\u00EDrculos, con n\u00FAmeros 1\u20139. Una secuencia no mezcla palos. Los terminales son 1 y 9; los simples son 2\u20138.",
              "- Los honores son los vientos Este, Sur, Oeste y Norte y los dragones blanco, verde y rojo. No tienen n\u00FAmero ni forman secuencias.",
              "- Un chow o chi son tres n\u00FAmeros consecutivos de un palo: bamb\u00FAes 3, 4 y 5. Bamb\u00FA 3, c\u00EDrculo 4 y bamb\u00FA 5 no sirven, ni tampoco 8, 9 y 1 del mismo palo.",
              "- Un pung, tambi\u00E9n pong o pon, son tres fichas id\u00E9nticas, como tres dragones rojos. Tres cincos de palos distintos no son pung.",
              "- Un kong o kan son cuatro fichas id\u00E9nticas. Debes declararlo con la acci\u00F3n de kong; tener las cuatro no lo declara. Cuenta como un grupo y permite un robo de reposici\u00F3n.",
              "- Una pareja son dos fichas id\u00E9nticas. Un grupo es chow, pung o kong. Una mano ganadora normal necesita cuatro grupos y una pareja; varias parejas o muchas fichas de un palo no bastan.",
              "- El muro es la reserva de fichas para robar. Robar a\u00F1ade una ficha a tu mano; descartar quita una y permite a los dem\u00E1s reclamarla.",
              "- Una mano cerrada no tiene grupos reclamados a rivales. Reclamar chow, pung o kong expuesto la abre. Los grupos de fichas propias pueden quedarse ocultos. Un kong oculto declarado no abre la mano.",
              "- Una victoria por robo propio usa la ficha que acabas de robar; una victoria por descarte usa la descartada por un rival. Elige Declarar Mahjong para ganar: no descartes antes la ficha ganadora.",
              "- Una espera es un tipo de ficha que completar\u00EDa la mano. Con bamb\u00FAes 4\u20135 puedes esperar 3 o 6. Tenpai significa estar a una ficha de una forma completa. Para ganar tambi\u00E9n debe cumplirse la condici\u00F3n de puntuaci\u00F3n de la variante.",
              "- Los fan son unidades del valor de la mano por patrones y circunstancias. Este perfil exige al menos 3 fan para ganar. Luego se convierten en puntos de partida: 3 fan no significa ganar solo 3 puntos.",
              "- El viento propio es tu funci\u00F3n: quien reparte es Este, seguido de Sur, Oeste y Norte. El viento de ronda es com\u00FAn. Un pung del viento propio o de ronda puede puntuar; una pareja no recibe ese premio.",
              "- Las flores y estaciones son bonos: el juego las expone y repone autom\u00E1ticamente. No ocupan un chow, pung ni la pareja y no indican rondas de vientos."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_start, "Hong Kong \u2014 Inicio de la mano",
              "Los saldos empiezan en cero; los pagos ganados suman puntos y los perdidos los restan.",
              "Quien reparte empieza con 14 fichas y descarta primero; los dem\u00E1s tienen 13. En un turno normal elige Robar una ficha y pulsa Enter. Recorrer la lista no roba. Si empiezas repartiendo, ya tienes la ficha adicional para descartar."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_play, "Hong Kong \u2014 Tus primeros turnos",
              "Antes del primer descarte, recorre tu mano con Arriba y Abajo. Busca n\u00FAmeros pr\u00F3ximos para chows, fichas iguales para pungs y una pareja final. No marcas por separado los grupos que conservas en la mano: el juego los reconoce al declarar victoria.",
              "Si est\u00E1s en los campos del juego, tu nuevo turno enfoca Robar una ficha. Tras robar, el foco pasa a la ficha nueva, marcada como reci\u00E9n robada. No la descarta. Puedes recorrer otras fichas, conservar la nueva y descartar una antigua.",
              "Para descartar, busca una ficha en la lista de descartes permitidos y pulsa Enter. Solo pierdes esa copia. Si ten\u00EDas tres iguales, las otras dos permanecen.",
              "Tras un descarte ajeno oir\u00E1s la ficha y ver\u00E1s respuestas. Pasar solo significa no reclamar esa ficha; no abandona ni concede la mano. Si todos pasan, roba el siguiente jugador. Puedes pasar varias veces antes de que te toque robar.",
              "El primer campo describe la situaci\u00F3n. Despu\u00E9s van todas las acciones disponibles: respuestas, robar, declarar Mahjong, kongs o Riichi cuando corresponda. Luego vienen tus fichas. Tab y Shift+Tab cambian de campo; las flechas eligen y Enter confirma. Las acciones no disponibles se omiten.",
              "Grupos expuestos muestra los grupos declarados de todos con el nombre de su due\u00F1o. Descartes indica qui\u00E9n descart\u00F3 cada ficha. Sirven para saber qu\u00E9 se ha usado y qu\u00E9 podr\u00EDan necesitar los rivales. Consultarlos no realiza una jugada.",
              "Si una ficha completa una mano apta, elige Declarar Mahjong en vez de descartarla o pasar. El juego comprueba forma y requisitos de puntuaci\u00F3n. Si antes se agota el muro, hay empate; no gana autom\u00E1ticamente quien est\u00E9 m\u00E1s cerca de completar su mano.",
              "S lee los puntos actuales de todos. M indica cu\u00E1ntas fichas quedan por robar en el muro. Solo informan: no roban, descartan ni pasan ni revelan manos ajenas.",
              "W lee el valor de una victoria v\u00E1lida: fan o han y fu, y los puntos ganados. Sin una victoria v\u00E1lida no indica el valor final de la mano. Antes de ganar en Riichi excluye las ura-dora ocultas, por lo que el valor final puede ser mayor. U lee tus grupos expuestos, May\u00FAs+U los de los dem\u00E1s y R el n\u00FAmero de mano, los vientos y la situaci\u00F3n actual. En Riichi, I lee los indicadores y fichas dora, tu cantidad de dora, las declaraciones de Riichi, los dep\u00F3sitos, honba y tu restricci\u00F3n furiten. Estas teclas solo leen informaci\u00F3n."),
            rule_section(:table_hand, "Hong Kong \u2014 Qu\u00E9 formar para ganar",
              "Forma cuatro chows, pungs o kongs y una pareja, contando grupos declarados y conservados. Un chow son tres n\u00FAmeros consecutivos de un palo, un pung tres fichas iguales, un kong cuatro y una pareja dos. Tambi\u00E9n necesitas 3 fan. Al cumplir ambas condiciones elige Declarar Mahjong y pulsa Enter. El cat\u00E1logo de puntuaci\u00F3n aparece despu\u00E9s; robar una ficha adecuada no termina la mano autom\u00E1ticamente."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_claims, "Hong Kong \u2014 Reclamar fichas y declarar kongs",
              "Cuando otro jugador descarta, la lista de respuestas muestra las acciones permitidas. Elige Pasar si no quieres la ficha. Para reclamarla, selecciona Chow, Pung, Kong o la declaraci\u00F3n de mahjong y pulsa Enter. Solo puedes reclamar un chow al jugador inmediatamente anterior. Un pung o kong puede usar el descarte de cualquier rival.",
              "Una declaraci\u00F3n de victoria tiene prioridad sobre una combinaci\u00F3n; pung y kong tienen prioridad sobre chow. Si varios jugadores pueden ganar con la misma ficha, gana el primero en el orden de turno despu\u00E9s de quien descart\u00F3. Se esperan todas las respuestas necesarias. Tras reclamar chow o pung, descarta sin robar; la combinaci\u00F3n queda expuesta.",
              "Despu\u00E9s de declarar un kong, roba una ficha de reemplazo antes de descartar. Si tienes las cuatro copias, puedes declararlas desde la lista de kongs ocultos. Tambi\u00E9n puedes a\u00F1adir una cuarta ficha a un pung expuesto. Antes de completarse ese kong, los rivales pueden declarar victoria con la ficha a\u00F1adida.",
              "Si tienes dos dragones rojos y alguien descarta otro, Pung toma la ficha y expone los tres. Luego descartas sin robar. Con bamb\u00FAes 4\u20135 puedes reclamar Chow con 3 o 6 solo del jugador inmediatamente anterior, no de otro rival."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_sets, "Hong Kong \u2014 cat\u00E1logo completo de puntuaci\u00F3n",
              "Una victoria normal requiere cuatro grupos y pareja y al menos 3 fan. Siete parejas y trece hu\u00E9rfanos usan otras formas. El cat\u00E1logo completo de esta versi\u00F3n aparece a continuaci\u00F3n; salvo restricci\u00F3n, admite manos abiertas y cerradas. Un chow normal aislado, pung num\u00E9rico o pareja no da un bono propio.",
              "Los bonos normales compatibles se suman. Los tres peque\u00F1os y grandes dragones sustituyen los bonos de sus pungs. Las manos especiales de 10 fan o m\u00E1s reciben solo el mayor premio especial aplicable, sin bonos normales. Una mano cerrada o cuatro pungs ocultos no tienen premio aparte. Las ocho fichas de bonificaci\u00F3n dan 4 fan; siete parejas dan 4 fan."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_1, "Hong Kong: 1 fan",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Robo propio: roba la ficha ganadora.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Todo chows: cuatro secuencias y una pareja, que puede ser de honores.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung o kong de dragones rojos.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung o kong de dragones verdes.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung o kong de dragones blancos.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung o kong del viento propio.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Pung o kong del viento de la ronda; si coincide con el propio, suma 2 fan en total.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Ganar con la reposici\u00F3n de un kong; tambi\u00E9n cuenta el robo propio.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Robar un kong: ganar con la ficha a\u00F1adida a un pung expuesto.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 \u00DAltimo robo normal del muro; tambi\u00E9n cuenta el robo propio.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Ganar con el \u00FAltimo descarte tras agotarse el muro normal.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Sin fichas de bonificaci\u00F3n: ninguna flor ni estaci\u00F3n.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Tu flor: su n\u00FAmero corresponde al viento propio.",
              "- 1 fan \u2014 Tu estaci\u00F3n: su n\u00FAmero corresponde al viento propio."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_2, "Hong Kong: 2 fan",
              "- 2 fan \u2014 Las cuatro flores: sustituye el fan por tu flor.",
              "- 2 fan \u2014 Las cuatro estaciones: sustituye el fan por tu estaci\u00F3n."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_3_8, "Hong Kong: 3\u20138 fan",
              "- 3 fan \u2014 Todo pungs: cuatro pungs o kongs y una pareja.",
              "- 3 fan \u2014 Palo mixto: un palo num\u00E9rico y honores, ambos presentes.",
              "- 4 fan \u2014 Siete parejas: cuatro fichas id\u00E9nticas sin declarar kong pueden formar dos parejas.",
              "- 5 fan \u2014 Tres peque\u00F1os dragones: dos pungs o kongs de dragones y pareja del tercero; incluye los bonos por pungs de dragones.",
              "- 7 fan \u2014 Palo puro: solo bamb\u00FAes, c\u00EDrculos o caracteres, sin honores.",
              "- 8 fan \u2014 Tres grandes dragones: pungs o kongs de los tres dragones; incluye los bonos por sus pungs."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_fan_special, "Hong Kong: 10\u201318 fan",
              "- 10 fan \u2014 Cuatro peque\u00F1os vientos: tres pungs o kongs de vientos y pareja del cuarto.",
              "- 10 fan \u2014 Todo honores: mano ganadora v\u00E1lida formada solo por vientos y dragones.",
              "- 10 fan \u2014 Todo terminales: solo unos y nueves, sin honores.",
              "- 13 fan \u2014 Trece hu\u00E9rfanos: cada 1 y 9, los cuatro vientos y tres dragones, m\u00E1s una repetida para la pareja; mano cerrada.",
              "- 15 fan \u2014 Cuatro grandes vientos: cuatro pungs o kongs de vientos y pareja.",
              "- 15 fan \u2014 Nueve puertas: base oculta exacta 1112345678999 de un palo antes de robar o reclamar otra ficha de ese palo.",
              "- 18 fan \u2014 Cuatro kongs y una pareja."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_bonus, "Hong Kong \u2014 flores y estaciones seg\u00FAn el viento",
              "Tu flor y estaci\u00F3n dependen del viento propio, no del de la ronda. El 1 es Este, 2 Sur, 3 Oeste y 4 Norte. Una familia completa sustituye su bono por ficha propia; las dos familias punt\u00FAan por separado.",
              "- Este: flor de ciruelo y primavera.",
              "- Sur: flor de orqu\u00EDdea y verano.",
              "- Oeste: flor de crisantemo y oto\u00F1o.",
              "- Norte: flor de bamb\u00FA y invierno."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_payments, "Hong Kong \u2014 Puntuaci\u00F3n y pagos",
              "Bases de pago: 3 fan \u2014 8 puntos; 4 \u2014 16; 5 \u2014 24; 6 \u2014 32; 7 \u2014 48; 8 o m\u00E1s \u2014 64. Una mano puede superar 8 fan, pero no aumenta su base de pago.",
              "Con descarte, quien descarta paga dos bases y los otros rivales una cada uno. Con robo propio, cada rival paga dos bases.",
              "Las manos celestial y terrenal no se premian actualmente durante la partida. No a\u00F1adas por ellas un bono de victoria inicial."),
            rule_section(:table_winds, "Hong Kong \u2014 Manos y duraci\u00F3n de la partida",
              "La partida predeterminada tiene cuatro rondas: Este, Sur, Oeste y Norte. Todos completan un per\u00EDodo de reparto en cada una, al menos diecis\u00E9is manos. Los pagos se acumulan desde cero; una victoria de mano no garantiza ganar la partida.",
              "Quien reparte repite tras su victoria o cualquier empate. Este dura al menos cuatro manos, a veces m\u00E1s. Cuatro rondas son Este, Sur, Oeste y Norte: al menos 16 manos. Las estaciones son bonos, no estas rondas.",
              "Tras la mano consulta ganador, valor y cambios de puntos. En partidas largas todos eligen estar listos para la siguiente. El empate no cambia puntos de mano en Hong Kong. Tras las rondas elegidas gana el mayor saldo; m\u00E1ximos iguales empatan."),
            rule_section(:table_hk_variants, "Hong Kong \u2014 Elegir la duraci\u00F3n",
              "Elige la duraci\u00F3n en las opciones antes de jugar. Los ajustes m\u00E1s cortos cambian el n\u00FAmero de manos, no c\u00F3mo se forma una mano ganadora.",
              "Una mano acaba tras una victoria o empate por muro agotado. Ronda Este significa que los cuatro completan su per\u00EDodo de reparto. Quien reparte es Este y el viento com\u00FAn sigue siendo Este durante esa ronda. No es un turno individual."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_aim, "Riichi \u2014 Objetivo y condiciones de victoria",
              "En la partida normal de cuatro buscas completar tu mano antes que los rivales. Suele tener cuatro grupos y pareja, pero necesita un yaku, una condici\u00F3n reconocida de victoria. Una forma completa sin yaku puede no ganar. Gana el mayor saldo final; el siguiente cap\u00EDtulo explica los t\u00E9rminos."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_terms, "Riichi \u2014 Significado de los t\u00E9rminos",
              "Juegas contra los dem\u00E1s. Formas una mano ganadora con tus fichas; no retiras parejas de un dise\u00F1o como en el solitario. Tu mano es el conjunto de tus fichas. Una mano de juego va desde el reparto hasta una victoria o empate; la partida puede contener varias.",
              "- Una ficha es una pieza, como bamb\u00FA 5. Su tipo es el palo y n\u00FAmero, o el viento o drag\u00F3n concreto. Hay cuatro copias de cada tipo normal.",
              "- Los palos son caracteres, bamb\u00FAes y c\u00EDrculos, con n\u00FAmeros 1\u20139. Una secuencia no mezcla palos. Los terminales son 1 y 9; los simples son 2\u20138.",
              "- Los honores son los vientos Este, Sur, Oeste y Norte y los dragones blanco, verde y rojo. No tienen n\u00FAmero ni forman secuencias.",
              "- Un chow o chi son tres n\u00FAmeros consecutivos de un palo: bamb\u00FAes 3, 4 y 5. Bamb\u00FA 3, c\u00EDrculo 4 y bamb\u00FA 5 no sirven, ni tampoco 8, 9 y 1 del mismo palo.",
              "- Un pung, tambi\u00E9n pong o pon, son tres fichas id\u00E9nticas, como tres dragones rojos. Tres cincos de palos distintos no son pung.",
              "- Un kong o kan son cuatro fichas id\u00E9nticas. Debes declararlo con la acci\u00F3n de kong; tener las cuatro no lo declara. Cuenta como un grupo y permite un robo de reposici\u00F3n.",
              "- Una pareja son dos fichas id\u00E9nticas. Un grupo es chow, pung o kong. Una mano ganadora normal necesita cuatro grupos y una pareja; varias parejas o muchas fichas de un palo no bastan.",
              "- El muro es la reserva de fichas para robar. Robar a\u00F1ade una ficha a tu mano; descartar quita una y permite a los dem\u00E1s reclamarla.",
              "- Una mano cerrada no tiene grupos reclamados a rivales. Reclamar chow, pung o kong expuesto la abre. Los grupos de fichas propias pueden quedarse ocultos. Un kong oculto declarado no abre la mano.",
              "- Una victoria por robo propio usa la ficha que acabas de robar; una victoria por descarte usa la descartada por un rival. Elige Declarar Mahjong para ganar: no descartes antes la ficha ganadora.",
              "- Una espera es un tipo de ficha que completar\u00EDa la mano. Con bamb\u00FAes 4\u20135 puedes esperar 3 o 6. Tenpai significa estar a una ficha de una forma completa. Para ganar tambi\u00E9n debe cumplirse la condici\u00F3n de puntuaci\u00F3n de la variante.",
              "- Un yaku es una condici\u00F3n con nombre que permite ganar con una forma completa. Necesitas al menos uno. Un pung de dragones es yaku; una forma completa cualquiera puede no tener ninguno.",
              "- Los han miden el valor de yaku y bonos. Los fu son puntos menores por grupos, pareja y espera. Juntos determinan el pago. Los fu por s\u00ED solos no sustituyen el yaku exigido.",
              "- Ron es ganar con descarte ajeno; tsumo, con robo propio. Menzen significa cerrado. Menzen tsumo es ganar por robo propio con mano cerrada y constituye un yaku.",
              "- Riichi declara que a tu mano cerrada le falta una ficha. Depositas 1000 puntos y obtienes un yaku, pero restringe los cambios posteriores.",
              "- Dora es un tipo de ficha de bonificaci\u00F3n indicado por un marcador. Cada copia a\u00F1ade han cuando ya tienes yaku. Solo dora no permite ganar.",
              "- Furiten restringe ganar con descartes ajenos. Puede venir de tus descartes o de rechazar un descarte ganador. No impide ganar por robo propio.",
              "- Honba es el contador de pagos extra tras empates o repeticiones. Mangan y l\u00EDmites superiores son niveles de pago de manos valiosas, no grupos adicionales que debas formar.",
              "- El viento propio depende de quien reparte: Este para \u00E9l, luego Sur, Oeste y Norte. El viento de ronda es com\u00FAn. Un pung de cualquiera de los dos puede puntuar; si coinciden, un grupo cumple ambas condiciones."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_start, "Riichi \u2014 Inicio de la mano",
              "Todos empiezan con 30 000 puntos. Los pagos y dep\u00F3sitos Riichi aceptados cambian ese saldo.",
              "Quien reparte empieza con 14 fichas y descarta primero; los dem\u00E1s tienen 13. En un turno normal elige Robar una ficha y pulsa Enter. Recorrer la lista no roba. Si empiezas repartiendo, ya tienes la ficha adicional para descartar."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_play, "Riichi \u2014 Tus primeros turnos",
              "Antes del primer descarte, recorre tu mano con Arriba y Abajo. Busca n\u00FAmeros pr\u00F3ximos para chows, fichas iguales para pungs y una pareja final. No marcas por separado los grupos que conservas en la mano: el juego los reconoce al declarar victoria.",
              "Si est\u00E1s en los campos del juego, tu nuevo turno enfoca Robar una ficha. Tras robar, el foco pasa a la ficha nueva, marcada como reci\u00E9n robada. No la descarta. Puedes recorrer otras fichas, conservar la nueva y descartar una antigua.",
              "Para descartar, busca una ficha en la lista de descartes permitidos y pulsa Enter. Solo pierdes esa copia. Si ten\u00EDas tres iguales, las otras dos permanecen.",
              "Tras un descarte ajeno oir\u00E1s la ficha y ver\u00E1s respuestas. Pasar solo significa no reclamar esa ficha; no abandona ni concede la mano. Si todos pasan, roba el siguiente jugador. Puedes pasar varias veces antes de que te toque robar.",
              "El primer campo describe la situaci\u00F3n. Despu\u00E9s van todas las acciones disponibles: respuestas, robar, declarar Mahjong, kongs o Riichi cuando corresponda. Luego vienen tus fichas. Tab y Shift+Tab cambian de campo; las flechas eligen y Enter confirma. Las acciones no disponibles se omiten.",
              "Grupos expuestos muestra los grupos declarados de todos con el nombre de su due\u00F1o. Descartes indica qui\u00E9n descart\u00F3 cada ficha. Sirven para saber qu\u00E9 se ha usado y qu\u00E9 podr\u00EDan necesitar los rivales. Consultarlos no realiza una jugada.",
              "Si una ficha completa una mano apta, elige Declarar Mahjong en vez de descartarla o pasar. El juego comprueba forma y requisitos de puntuaci\u00F3n. Si antes se agota el muro, hay empate; no gana autom\u00E1ticamente quien est\u00E9 m\u00E1s cerca de completar su mano.",
              "S lee los puntos actuales de todos. M indica cu\u00E1ntas fichas quedan por robar en el muro. Solo informan: no roban, descartan ni pasan ni revelan manos ajenas.",
              "W lee el valor de una victoria v\u00E1lida: fan o han y fu, y los puntos ganados. Sin una victoria v\u00E1lida no indica el valor final de la mano. Antes de ganar en Riichi excluye las ura-dora ocultas, por lo que el valor final puede ser mayor. U lee tus grupos expuestos, May\u00FAs+U los de los dem\u00E1s y R el n\u00FAmero de mano, los vientos y la situaci\u00F3n actual. En Riichi, I lee los indicadores y fichas dora, tu cantidad de dora, las declaraciones de Riichi, los dep\u00F3sitos, honba y tu restricci\u00F3n furiten. Estas teclas solo leen informaci\u00F3n."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_build, "Riichi \u2014 Qu\u00E9 formar para ganar",
              "La forma normal es cuatro grupos y una pareja. Las alternativas son siete parejas distintas y trece hu\u00E9rfanos. Para ganar necesitas yaku; solo dora y fu no bastan. Cuando haya victoria v\u00E1lida elige Declarar Mahjong y pulsa Enter. Los cat\u00E1logos siguientes incluyen todos los yaku premiados y los valores de fu."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_claims, "Riichi \u2014 Reclamar fichas y declarar kongs",
              "Cuando otro jugador descarta, la lista de respuestas muestra las acciones permitidas. Elige Pasar si no quieres la ficha. Para reclamarla, selecciona Chow, Pung, Kong o la declaraci\u00F3n de mahjong y pulsa Enter. Solo puedes reclamar un chow al jugador inmediatamente anterior. Un pung o kong puede usar el descarte de cualquier rival.",
              "Una declaraci\u00F3n de victoria tiene prioridad sobre una combinaci\u00F3n; pung y kong tienen prioridad sobre chow. Si varios jugadores pueden ganar con la misma ficha, gana el primero en el orden de turno despu\u00E9s de quien descart\u00F3. Se esperan todas las respuestas necesarias. Tras reclamar chow o pung, descarta sin robar; la combinaci\u00F3n queda expuesta.",
              "Despu\u00E9s de declarar un kong, roba una ficha de reemplazo antes de descartar. Si tienes las cuatro copias, puedes declararlas desde la lista de kongs ocultos. Tambi\u00E9n puedes a\u00F1adir una cuarta ficha a un pung expuesto. Antes de completarse ese kong, los rivales pueden declarar victoria con la ficha a\u00F1adida.",
              "Abrir puede eliminar el yaku planeado. Riichi y robo propio cerrado exigen mano cerrada; los pungs de dragones punt\u00FAan abiertos. Antes de reclamar piensa qu\u00E9 yaku quedar\u00E1."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi, "Riichi \u2014 declarar tenpai, dora y furiten",
              "Si a tu mano cerrada le falta una ficha para ganar, la lista Riichi ofrece los descartes que permiten declarar que est\u00E1s preparado. Elige uno y pulsa Enter. La declaraci\u00F3n aceptada deposita 1 000 puntos en la mesa. Despu\u00E9s debes descartar la ficha robada salvo que ganes; solo se permite un kong que conserve todas las interpretaciones exigidas. El ganador recoge los dep\u00F3sitos; los no reclamados pasan a la siguiente mano.",
              "Furiten impide ganar por descarte si alguna ficha que completar\u00EDa tu mano aparece entre tus propios descartes. Pasar una victoria v\u00E1lida sobre una ficha ajena tambi\u00E9n bloquea temporalmente esas victorias; tras declarar Riichi, el bloqueo dura hasta el final de la mano. A\u00FAn puedes ganar por robo propio.",
              "Ejemplo de furiten: esperas bamb\u00FA 3 o 6 pero antes descartaste 3. Se bloquea ganar por descarte con ambos, tambi\u00E9n con 6, no solo con la ficha descartada. Robar t\u00FA 3 o 6 a\u00FAn puede ganar."),
            rule_section(:table_yaku, "Riichi \u2014 cat\u00E1logo completo de yaku y han",
              "Riichi requiere una mano v\u00E1lida y al menos un yaku. Se indican todos los yaku normales premiados en esta versi\u00F3n, con sus valores para mano cerrada y abierta. Un kong oculto no abre la mano. Un pung completado por el descarte ganador no se considera oculto al puntuar.",
              "- Riichi: mano cerrada preparada y declaraci\u00F3n aceptada con dep\u00F3sito de 1000 puntos. Solo cerrada: 1 han.",
              "- Doble riichi: declaraci\u00F3n en la primera vuelta sin interrupciones; sustituye al riichi normal. Solo cerrada: 2 han.",
              "- Ippatsu: ganar en la primera vuelta sin interrupciones tras riichi. Solo cerrada: 1 han.",
              "- Menzen tsumo: robo propio con mano cerrada. Solo cerrada: 1 han.",
              "- Tanyao: solo n\u00FAmeros del 2 al 8, sin honores. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Yakuhai: cada pung o kong de dragones por separado. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Pung o kong del viento propio. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Pung o kong del viento de la ronda; si coincide con el propio, 2 han en total. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Haitei: ganar con el \u00FAltimo robo normal del muro. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Houtei: ganar con el \u00FAltimo descarte. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Rinshan: ganar con la reposici\u00F3n de un kong. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Chankan: robar un kong. Cerrada: 1 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Pinfu: cuatro chows, pareja sin valor y espera de dos lados. Solo cerrada: 1 han.",
              "- Iipeikou: dos chows id\u00E9nticos de un palo. Solo cerrada: 1 han.",
              "- Ryanpeikou: dos parejas de chows id\u00E9nticos; sustituye a iipeikou. Solo cerrada: 3 han.",
              "- Chiitoitsu: siete parejas distintas; cuatro fichas id\u00E9nticas no forman dos parejas. Solo cerrada: 2 han.",
              "- Toitoi: cuatro pungs o kongs y pareja. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Sanankou: tres pungs o kongs ocultos; el cuarto grupo puede estar expuesto. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Sankantsu: tres kongs. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Shousangen: dos pungs o kongs de dragones y pareja del tercero; tambi\u00E9n cuentan ambos yakuhai. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Sanshoku doujun: el mismo chow en los tres palos. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Sanshoku doukou: pungs del mismo n\u00FAmero en los tres palos. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Ittsu: chows 123, 456 y 789 de un palo. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Chanta: cada grupo y pareja contiene un terminal u honor, con chows y honores presentes. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 1 han.",
              "- Junchan: cada grupo y pareja contiene un 1 o 9; hay chows y no hay honores. Cerrada: 3 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Honroutou: solo terminales y honores. Cerrada: 2 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Honitsu: un palo num\u00E9rico y honores. Cerrada: 3 han; abierta: 2 han.",
              "- Chinitsu: un palo num\u00E9rico sin honores. Cerrada: 6 han; abierta: 5 han."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_dora, "Riichi \u2014 han adicionales por dora",
              "Las dora suman 1 han por cada ficha dora propia, solo si la mano tiene un yaku real. No son yaku. El indicador se\u00F1ala la siguiente ficha de su ciclo; cuenta cada copia en la mano o grupos propios. Tras un riichi aceptado, las ura-dora ocultas pueden sumarse a la victoria final; la vista previa no las revela."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_yakuman, "Riichi \u2014 cat\u00E1logo completo de yakuman",
              "Cada entrada da 1 yakuman y sustituye los han y fu normales. Distintos yakuman compatibles pueden sumarse. Esta versi\u00F3n no duplica un yakuman por una espera especial ni por cuatro grandes vientos.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Trece hu\u00E9rfanos: todos los terminales y honores y una ficha repetida; mano cerrada.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Cuatro pungs o kongs ocultos: al ganar con descarte, este debe completar la pareja.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Tres grandes dragones: pungs o kongs de los tres dragones.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Cuatro grandes vientos: cuatro pungs o kongs de vientos.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Cuatro peque\u00F1os vientos: tres pungs o kongs de vientos y pareja del cuarto.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Todo honores: solo vientos y dragones.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Todo terminales: solo unos y nueves.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Todo verde: solo bamb\u00FAes 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 y dragones verdes.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Cuatro kongs y pareja.",
              "- 1 yakuman \u2014 Nueve puertas: mano cerrada de un palo, 1112345678999 y una ficha adicional de ese palo.",
              "Las manos celestial y terrenal no se premian actualmente durante la partida. No a\u00F1adas por ellas un bono de victoria inicial."),
            rule_section(:table_fu, "Riichi: fu por grupos y esperas",
              "Los han valoran los yaku y las bonificaciones; los fu valoran la construcci\u00F3n de la mano. Un chow da 0 fu. Una pareja normal tambi\u00E9n da 0; una pareja de dragones, del viento propio o del viento de ronda da 2. En este juego sigue dando 2 fu si el viento propio coincide con el de ronda.",
              "El valor de pungs y kongs depende de las fichas y de si est\u00E1n expuestos:",
              "- Pung de simples, n\u00FAmeros 2\u20138: expuesto 2 fu, oculto 4 fu.",
              "- Pung de terminales u honores: expuesto 4 fu, oculto 8 fu.",
              "- Kong de simples: expuesto 8 fu, oculto 16 fu.",
              "- Kong de terminales u honores: expuesto 16 fu, oculto 32 fu.",
              "El pung completado con el descarte ganador cuenta como expuesto para los fu. Los dem\u00E1s pungs conservados en la mano siguen ocultos. A\u00F1adir la cuarta ficha a un pung expuesto forma un kong expuesto.",
              "El c\u00E1lculo normal empieza en 20 fu. Ganar por descarte con mano cerrada a\u00F1ade 10; ganar por robo propio a\u00F1ade 2. Esperar la pareja, el centro o el borde de una secuencia a\u00F1ade 2. Borde significa esperar 3 con 1\u20132, o 7 con 8\u20139; centro, por ejemplo, 5 con 4\u20136. Una espera por dos lados, como 3 o 6 con 4\u20135, no a\u00F1ade fu.",
              "Redondea el total hacia arriba a decenas: 32 pasa a 40 fu. Siete parejas siempre vale 25 fu. Pinfu por robo propio vale 20 fu sin los 2 adicionales del robo. Cualquier otra mano que solo tuviera 20 recibe un m\u00EDnimo de 30.",
              "Por ejemplo: 20 de base, 10 por victoria cerrada por descarte, 4 por un pung oculto de cincos y 2 por pareja de dragones suman 36, que se redondea a 40 fu. Sigue haciendo falta un yaku: los fu por s\u00ED solos no permiten ganar."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_payments, "Riichi: convertir han y fu en puntos",
              "Para una victoria normal la base es fu por 2 elevado a han m\u00E1s 2, con l\u00EDmite de 2000. Con 1 han y 30 fu es 30 por 8, es decir 240. Cada pago final se redondea hacia arriba a centenas.",
              "Por descarte, quien gana sin repartir recibe cuatro bases de quien descarta; quien reparte recibe seis. Por robo propio, quien no reparte recibe dos bases de quien reparte y una de cada otro jugador. Si gana quien reparte, todos pagan dos bases.",
              "Con 1 han 30 fu sin bonos, quien no reparte gana 1000 por descarte, o 500 de quien reparte y 300 de cada otro rival por robo propio, total 1100. Quien reparte gana 1500 por descarte o 500 de cada rival, tambi\u00E9n 1500.",
              "- Mangan: 5 han o alcanzar el l\u00EDmite de base; aqu\u00ED tambi\u00E9n 3 han 60 fu y 4 han 30 fu. Por descarte son 8000 sin repartir o 12 000 repartiendo.",
              "- Haneman: 6\u20137 han, respectivamente 12 000 o 18 000 por descarte.",
              "- Baiman: 8\u201310 han, 16 000 o 24 000.",
              "- Sanbaiman: 11\u201312 han, 24 000 o 36 000.",
              "- Yakuman o al menos 13 han: 32 000 o 48 000. Varios yakuman compatibles multiplican ese pago.",
              "Cada honba a\u00F1ade 300 al pago por descarte o 100 de cada rival por robo propio. Quien gana tambi\u00E9n recibe los dep\u00F3sitos de Riichi. Honba aumenta tras un empate o repetici\u00F3n del reparto y vuelve a cero cuando gana otro jugador."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_match, "Riichi \u2014 Manos y duraci\u00F3n de la partida",
              "Por defecto juegan cuatro con 30 000 puntos y rondas Este y Sur. Cada uno completa su reparto en ambas: m\u00EDnimo ocho manos, m\u00E1s con repeticiones. Los pagos y dep\u00F3sitos Riichi aceptados cambian los saldos durante la partida.",
              "Quien reparte repite tras su victoria o un empate de muro si est\u00E1 en tenpai.",
              "En empate de muro se transfieren 3000 puntos de noten a tenpai, salvo que todos o ninguno est\u00E9n listos. Despu\u00E9s todos confirman la siguiente mano. Se juegan las rondas elegidas; aqu\u00ED un saldo negativo o l\u00EDder que reparte al final no termina antes. Gana el mayor saldo; m\u00E1ximos iguales empatan."),
            rule_section(:table_riichi_variants, "Riichi \u2014 variante de tres y duraci\u00F3n",
              "Elige la duraci\u00F3n en las opciones antes de jugar. Los ajustes m\u00E1s cortos cambian el n\u00FAmero de manos, no c\u00F3mo se forma una mano ganadora.",
              "Una mano acaba con victoria o empate por muro agotado. Ronda Este da a cada uno un per\u00EDodo de reparto: m\u00EDnimo cuatro manos con cuatro o tres en sanma. Este y Sur son dos rondas: m\u00EDnimo ocho o seis. Quien reparte repite tras ganar o empatar en tenpai, as\u00ED que puede haber m\u00E1s manos.",
              "Los vientos propios siguen desde quien reparte: Este, Sur, Oeste y Norte con cuatro. El de ronda sigue Este hasta completar todos el reparto, luego Sur en dos rondas. Los vientos de valor pueden cambiar. Sanma elimina caracteres 2\u20138 y esta versi\u00F3n no tiene extracci\u00F3n separada de Norte.",
              "En sanma no puedes reclamar Chow, aunque s\u00ED formar secuencias con tus fichas.",
              "En sanma el cuarto jugador ausente no paga y su parte no se redistribuye. Por eso el robo propio puede ganar menos que con cuatro jugadores. Los empates reparten 2000 entre tenpai y noten, en vez de 3000."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_aim, "MCR \u2014 Objetivo y condiciones de victoria",
              "Cuatro jugadores intentan completar primero una mano ganadora. La forma normal son cuatro grupos y pareja con al menos 8 fan sin flores. Los fan valoran patrones compatibles. Ganar una mano da un pago; gana la partida el mayor saldo acumulado."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_terms, "MCR \u2014 Significado de los t\u00E9rminos",
              "Juegas contra los dem\u00E1s. Formas una mano ganadora con tus fichas; no retiras parejas de un dise\u00F1o como en el solitario. Tu mano es el conjunto de tus fichas. Una mano de juego va desde el reparto hasta una victoria o empate; la partida puede contener varias.",
              "- Una ficha es una pieza, como bamb\u00FA 5. Su tipo es el palo y n\u00FAmero, o el viento o drag\u00F3n concreto. Hay cuatro copias de cada tipo normal.",
              "- Los palos son caracteres, bamb\u00FAes y c\u00EDrculos, con n\u00FAmeros 1\u20139. Una secuencia no mezcla palos. Los terminales son 1 y 9; los simples son 2\u20138.",
              "- Los honores son los vientos Este, Sur, Oeste y Norte y los dragones blanco, verde y rojo. No tienen n\u00FAmero ni forman secuencias.",
              "- Un chow o chi son tres n\u00FAmeros consecutivos de un palo: bamb\u00FAes 3, 4 y 5. Bamb\u00FA 3, c\u00EDrculo 4 y bamb\u00FA 5 no sirven, ni tampoco 8, 9 y 1 del mismo palo.",
              "- Un pung, tambi\u00E9n pong o pon, son tres fichas id\u00E9nticas, como tres dragones rojos. Tres cincos de palos distintos no son pung.",
              "- Un kong o kan son cuatro fichas id\u00E9nticas. Debes declararlo con la acci\u00F3n de kong; tener las cuatro no lo declara. Cuenta como un grupo y permite un robo de reposici\u00F3n.",
              "- Una pareja son dos fichas id\u00E9nticas. Un grupo es chow, pung o kong. Una mano ganadora normal necesita cuatro grupos y una pareja; varias parejas o muchas fichas de un palo no bastan.",
              "- El muro es la reserva de fichas para robar. Robar a\u00F1ade una ficha a tu mano; descartar quita una y permite a los dem\u00E1s reclamarla.",
              "- Una mano cerrada no tiene grupos reclamados a rivales. Reclamar chow, pung o kong expuesto la abre. Los grupos de fichas propias pueden quedarse ocultos. Un kong oculto declarado no abre la mano.",
              "- Una victoria por robo propio usa la ficha que acabas de robar; una victoria por descarte usa la descartada por un rival. Elige Declarar Mahjong para ganar: no descartes antes la ficha ganadora.",
              "- Una espera es un tipo de ficha que completar\u00EDa la mano. Con bamb\u00FAes 4\u20135 puedes esperar 3 o 6. Tenpai significa estar a una ficha de una forma completa. Para ganar tambi\u00E9n debe cumplirse la condici\u00F3n de puntuaci\u00F3n de la variante.",
              "- MCR significa Mahjong Competition Rules. Los fan miden patrones puntuables. Necesitas al menos 8 fan antes de los bonos de flores. Luego sirven para calcular pagos; no son tu saldo total de partida.",
              "- Un patr\u00F3n describe una caracter\u00EDstica de la mano o sus grupos. Algunos se combinan; otros incluyen uno menor y excluyen sus puntos extra. No sumes todos los nombres que parezcan encajar.",
              "- Las flores y estaciones se exponen y reponen autom\u00E1ticamente. Cada una a\u00F1ade 1 fan a una victoria v\u00E1lida, pero no ayuda a alcanzar los 8. Siete fan y una flor no permiten ganar.",
              "- El viento propio es tu funci\u00F3n respecto a quien reparte; el de ronda es com\u00FAn. Un pung de cualquiera de esos vientos puede puntuar. Las estaciones son bonos, no las cuatro rondas de vientos."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_start, "MCR \u2014 Inicio de la mano",
              "Los saldos empiezan en cero; los pagos ganados suman puntos y los perdidos los restan.",
              "Quien reparte empieza con 14 fichas y descarta primero; los dem\u00E1s tienen 13. En un turno normal elige Robar una ficha y pulsa Enter. Recorrer la lista no roba. Si empiezas repartiendo, ya tienes la ficha adicional para descartar."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_play, "MCR \u2014 Tus primeros turnos",
              "Antes del primer descarte, recorre tu mano con Arriba y Abajo. Busca n\u00FAmeros pr\u00F3ximos para chows, fichas iguales para pungs y una pareja final. No marcas por separado los grupos que conservas en la mano: el juego los reconoce al declarar victoria.",
              "Si est\u00E1s en los campos del juego, tu nuevo turno enfoca Robar una ficha. Tras robar, el foco pasa a la ficha nueva, marcada como reci\u00E9n robada. No la descarta. Puedes recorrer otras fichas, conservar la nueva y descartar una antigua.",
              "Para descartar, busca una ficha en la lista de descartes permitidos y pulsa Enter. Solo pierdes esa copia. Si ten\u00EDas tres iguales, las otras dos permanecen.",
              "Tras un descarte ajeno oir\u00E1s la ficha y ver\u00E1s respuestas. Pasar solo significa no reclamar esa ficha; no abandona ni concede la mano. Si todos pasan, roba el siguiente jugador. Puedes pasar varias veces antes de que te toque robar.",
              "El primer campo describe la situaci\u00F3n. Despu\u00E9s van todas las acciones disponibles: respuestas, robar, declarar Mahjong, kongs o Riichi cuando corresponda. Luego vienen tus fichas. Tab y Shift+Tab cambian de campo; las flechas eligen y Enter confirma. Las acciones no disponibles se omiten.",
              "Grupos expuestos muestra los grupos declarados de todos con el nombre de su due\u00F1o. Descartes indica qui\u00E9n descart\u00F3 cada ficha. Sirven para saber qu\u00E9 se ha usado y qu\u00E9 podr\u00EDan necesitar los rivales. Consultarlos no realiza una jugada.",
              "Si una ficha completa una mano apta, elige Declarar Mahjong en vez de descartarla o pasar. El juego comprueba forma y requisitos de puntuaci\u00F3n. Si antes se agota el muro, hay empate; no gana autom\u00E1ticamente quien est\u00E9 m\u00E1s cerca de completar su mano.",
              "S lee los puntos actuales de todos. M indica cu\u00E1ntas fichas quedan por robar en el muro. Solo informan: no roban, descartan ni pasan ni revelan manos ajenas.",
              "W lee el valor de una victoria v\u00E1lida: fan o han y fu, y los puntos ganados. Sin una victoria v\u00E1lida no indica el valor final de la mano. Antes de ganar en Riichi excluye las ura-dora ocultas, por lo que el valor final puede ser mayor. U lee tus grupos expuestos, May\u00FAs+U los de los dem\u00E1s y R el n\u00FAmero de mano, los vientos y la situaci\u00F3n actual. En Riichi, I lee los indicadores y fichas dora, tu cantidad de dora, las declaraciones de Riichi, los dep\u00F3sitos, honba y tu restricci\u00F3n furiten. Estas teclas solo leen informaci\u00F3n."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_build, "MCR \u2014 Qu\u00E9 formar para ganar",
              "La forma normal es cuatro grupos y una pareja; MCR tambi\u00E9n permite siete parejas, trece hu\u00E9rfanos y las manos tejidas del cat\u00E1logo. Necesitas al menos 8 fan sin flores. Al cumplir las condiciones elige Declarar Mahjong y pulsa Enter. Cada entrada indica nombre, valor y condici\u00F3n de exposici\u00F3n."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_claims, "MCR \u2014 Reclamar fichas y declarar kongs",
              "Cuando otro jugador descarta, la lista de respuestas muestra las acciones permitidas. Elige Pasar si no quieres la ficha. Para reclamarla, selecciona Chow, Pung, Kong o la declaraci\u00F3n de mahjong y pulsa Enter. Solo puedes reclamar un chow al jugador inmediatamente anterior. Un pung o kong puede usar el descarte de cualquier rival.",
              "Una declaraci\u00F3n de victoria tiene prioridad sobre una combinaci\u00F3n; pung y kong tienen prioridad sobre chow. Si varios jugadores pueden ganar con la misma ficha, gana el primero en el orden de turno despu\u00E9s de quien descart\u00F3. Se esperan todas las respuestas necesarias. Tras reclamar chow o pung, descarta sin robar; la combinaci\u00F3n queda expuesta.",
              "Despu\u00E9s de declarar un kong, roba una ficha de reemplazo antes de descartar. Si tienes las cuatro copias, puedes declararlas desde la lista de kongs ocultos. Tambi\u00E9n puedes a\u00F1adir una cuarta ficha a un pung expuesto. Antes de completarse ese kong, los rivales pueden declarar victoria con la ficha a\u00F1adida.",
              "Si tienes dos dragones blancos y se descarta otro, Pung los expone y asegura un patr\u00F3n de 2 fan. El resto de la mano a\u00FAn debe llegar a 8. Reclamar cambia los patrones de mano cerrada disponibles. Que Pung est\u00E9 disponible no garantiza el m\u00EDnimo futuro."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_sets, "MCR \u2014 c\u00F3mo leer el cat\u00E1logo de 81 entradas",
              "Las siguientes 81 entradas describen los elementos puntuables de MCR. Necesitas una forma v\u00E1lida y al menos 8 fan sin flores. Un kong tambi\u00E9n cumple un requisito de pung del mismo tipo. Los palos num\u00E9ricos son caracteres, c\u00EDrculos y bamb\u00FAes; los honores son vientos y dragones.",
              "Una mano cerrada no tiene grupos reclamados de descartes; declarar un kong oculto no la abre. Un pung completado por el descarte ganador se considera expuesto; los dem\u00E1s pungs conservados siguen ocultos. La base tejida usa 147 de un palo, 258 de otro y 369 del tercero, con cualquier asignaci\u00F3n de palos.",
              "No sumes todos los nombres coincidentes sin comprobar las exclusiones. Un patr\u00F3n mayor puede incluir otro menor y no se pueden reutilizar libremente los mismos grupos para bonos compuestos. El juego selecciona patrones compatibles. Los n\u00FAmeros de las exclusiones remiten al cat\u00E1logo MCR, no a una secuencia de jugadas."),
            rule_section(:table_mcr_fan_88, "MCR: 88 fan",
              "- MCR 1: 88 fan \u2014 Cuatro grandes vientos: cuatro pungs de vientos. Mano abierta o cerrada.",
